{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengliang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minglai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Minglai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Rahimi","name":"Rahimi, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihai","familyName":"Surdeanu","name":"Surdeanu, Mihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Answer Lies Within: Self-Derived Rewards Enable Explainable Relation Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:53:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-16T14:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:38:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-13T04:01:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:20:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-21T08:05:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-22T00:45:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the remarkable reasoning capabilities of large language models, they still struggle with one-shot relation extraction without predefined relation labels. We identify two pitfalls: models are often misled by irrelevant tokens instead of relation-conveying semantics, and they often fail to align with the abstraction level human annotators expect. We introduce a novel framework that closes this gap with two components: (1) COGRE, a cognitively-inspired reasoning framework that structures RE into a series of processes mimicking human text-processing; and (2) HIT@DICT, a reinforcement learning intermediate reward strategy that encourages reasoning to align with relational labels by rewarding relation-relevant phrases in reasoning. The reward is derived on a credit dictionary automatically extracted from correct predictions. Our experiments show that our framework improves both accuracy and explanation quality by addressing these two pitfalls. For example, COGRE with Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct on One-shot NYT29 achieves 24.65% F1, surpassing prior reasoning-based designs. Optimizing this approach with RL using HIT@DICT further improves performance by +23.46% points. Finally, human evaluation shows that our best model generates relational phrases closely aligned with gold labels, increasing human explanation quality ratings by 54% (relative)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:00:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Bal-Ghaoui","name":"Bal-Ghaoui, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fayssal","familyName":"Sabri","name":"Sabri, Fayssal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-FS-Agent: A Deliberative Role-based Large Language Model Architecture for Transparent Feature Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:25:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.11591/ijai.v15.i5.pp4026-4038","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-dimensional data remains a pervasive challenge in machine learning, often undermining model interpretability and computational efficiency. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise for dimensionality reduction through feature selection, existing LLM-based approaches frequently lack structured reasoning and transparent justification for their decisions. This paper introduces LLM-FS-Agent, a novel multi-agent architecture designed for interpretable and robust feature selection. The system orchestrates a deliberative \"debate\" among multiple LLM agents, each assigned a specific role, enabling collective evaluation of feature relevance and generation of detailed justifications. We evaluate LLM-FS-Agent in the cybersecurity domain using the CIC-DIAD 2024 IoT intrusion detection dataset and compare its performance against strong baselines, including LLM-Select and traditional methods such as PCA. Experimental results demonstrate that LLM-FS-Agent consistently achieves superior or comparable classification performance while reducing downstream training time by an average of 46% (statistically significant improvement, p = 0.028 for XGBoost). These findings highlight that the proposed deliberative architecture enhances both decision transparency and computational efficiency, establishing LLM-FS-Agent as a practical and reliable solution for real-world applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:30Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"May","name":"May, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diganta","familyName":"Misra","name":"Misra, Diganta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anjali","familyName":"Sridhar","name":"Sridhar, Anjali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justine","familyName":"Gehring","name":"Gehring, Justine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio Soares Ribeiro","familyName":"Junior","name":"Junior, Silvio Soares Ribeiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FreshBrew: A Benchmark for Evaluating AI Agents on Java Code Migration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:39:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:19:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-03T01:00:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:25:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3744916.3773214","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI coding assistants are rapidly becoming integral to modern software development. A key challenge in this space is the continual need to migrate and modernize codebases in response to evolving software ecosystems. Traditionally, such migrations have relied on rule-based systems and human intervention. With the advent of powerful large language models (LLMs), AI-driven agentic frameworks offer a promising alternative-but their effectiveness has not been systematically evaluated. In this paper, we introduce \\FreshBrew{}\\footnote{\\url{https://github.com/mrcabbage972/freshbrew}}, a novel benchmark for evaluating AI agents on project-level Java migrations, with a specific focus on measuring an agent's ability to preserve program semantics and avoid reward hacking, which we argue requires projects with high test coverage for a rigorous and reliable evaluation. We benchmark several state-of-the-art LLMs, and compare their performance against established rule-based tools. Our evaluation on a challenging subset of 228 public Maven repositories that build on JDK 8, fail on JDK 17, and have high test coverage shows that the top-performing model, Gemini 2.5 Flash, can successfully migrate 52.3\\% of projects to JDK 17. Our empirical analysis reveals novel insights into the critical strengths and limitations of current agentic approaches, offering actionable insights into their real-world applicability. Our empirical study reveals failure modes of current AI agents in realistic Java modernization tasks, providing a foundation for evaluating trustworthy code-migration systems. By releasing \\FreshBrew{}, we aim to facilitate rigorous, reproducible evaluation and catalyze progress in AI-driven codebase modernization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:42:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06042","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06042","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06042","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Shang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xusheng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yanfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent+P: Guiding UI Agents via Symbolic Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:36:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T20:06:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-27T17:25:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:46:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based UI agents show great promise for UI automation but often hallucinate in long-horizon tasks due to their lack of understanding of the global UI transition structure. To address this, we introduce AGENT+P, a novel framework that leverages symbolic planning to guide LLM-based UI agents. Specifically, we model an app's UI transition structure as a UI Transition Graph (UTG), which allows us to reformulate the UI automation task as a pathfinding problem on the UTG. This further enables an off-the-shelf symbolic planner to generate a provably correct and optimal high-level plan, preventing the agent from redundant exploration and guiding the agent to achieve the automation goals. AGENT+P is designed as a plug-and-play framework to enhance existing UI agents. Evaluation on the AndroidWorld benchmark demonstrates that AGENT+P improves the success rates of state-of-the-art UI agents by up to 14.32% and reduces the action steps by 37.70%. Our code is available at: https://github.com/KiteFlyKid/agentp."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06042","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:01Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05504","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05504","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05504","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Jinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunchan Daniel","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Eunchan Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily Haneul","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Emily Haneul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caedon","familyName":"Geoffrey","name":"Geoffrey, Caedon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyoshin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Hyoshin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanism design and equilibrium analysis of smart contract-mediated resource allocation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Finance (q-fin.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:50:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:18:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T16:34:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T01:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-26T05:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:30:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3934/jimo.2026037","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decentralized coordination and digital contracting are becoming essential in complex industrial systems, yet existing approaches often rely on ad-hoc heuristics or purely technical blockchain implementations without a rigorous economic foundation. This study developed a mechanism-design framework for smart contract-mediated resource allocation that jointly embeds efficiency, fairness, and resilience in decentralized coordination. We modeled agent interactions as a contract-clearing game under shared capacity constraints, established the existence and uniqueness of equilibrium, and proposed a decentralized price-adjustment algorithm with provable convergence suitable for real-time operation. Performance was evaluated through extensive synthetic simulations and validated using a representative real-world dataset. In addition to controlled experiments, a long-horizon empirical analysis using financial and macroeconomic data from 2006 to 2025 examined the mechanism under major economic regimes and shock conditions. Results showed that the proposed mechanism consistently reduces inequality and cost while maintaining near-optimal efficiency and rapid recovery following shocks, demonstrating dynamic stability beyond steady state."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Journal of Industrial and Management Optimization, Volume 22, Issue 2, pp. 997-1033 (2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05504","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mallika","familyName":"Mainali","name":"Mainali, Mallika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsha","familyName":"Sureshbabu","name":"Sureshbabu, Harsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anik","familyName":"Sen","name":"Sen, Anik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher B.","familyName":"Rauch","name":"Rauch, Christopher B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah D.","familyName":"Reifsnyder","name":"Reifsnyder, Noah D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. T.","familyName":"Turner","name":"Turner, J. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Floyd","name":"Floyd, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Molineaux","name":"Molineaux, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosina O.","familyName":"Weber","name":"Weber, Rosina O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Classical AI vs. LLMs for Decision-Maker Alignment in Health Insurance Choices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:21:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-25T01:09:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As algorithmic decision-makers are increasingly applied to high-stakes domains, AI alignment research has evolved from a focus on universal value alignment to context-specific approaches that account for decision-maker attributes. Prior work on Decision-Maker Alignment (DMA) has explored two primary strategies: (1) classical AI methods integrating case-based reasoning, Bayesian reasoning, and naturalistic decision-making, and (2) large language model (LLM)-based methods leveraging prompt engineering. While both approaches have shown promise in limited domains such as medical triage, their generalizability to novel contexts remains underexplored. In this work, we implement a prior classical AI model and develop an LLM-based algorithmic decision-maker evaluated using a large reasoning model (GPT-5) and a non-reasoning model (GPT-4) with weighted self-consistency under a zero-shot prompting framework, as proposed in recent literature. We evaluate both approaches on a health insurance decision-making dataset annotated for three target decision-makers with varying levels of risk tolerance (0.0, 0.5, 1.0). In the experiments reported herein, classical AI and LLM-based models achieved comparable alignment with attribute-based targets, with classical AI exhibiting slightly better alignment for a moderate risk profile. The dataset and open-source implementation are publicly available at: https://github.com/TeX-Base/ClassicalAIvsLLMsforDMAlignment and https://github.com/Parallax-Advanced-Research/ITM/tree/feature_insurance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures. Accepted at the Twelfth Annual Conference on Advances in Cognitive Systems (ACS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-25T02:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jieyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sneh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Sneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Weitekamp","name":"Weitekamp, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher J.","familyName":"MacLellan","name":"MacLellan, Christopher J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Should Users Check? Modeling Confirmation Frequency in Multi-Step Agentic AI Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:18:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:06:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-08T02:11:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:52:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-07T15:51:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-08T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T17:30:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-16T01:18:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3790655","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing AI agents typically execute multi-step tasks autonomously and only allow user confirmation at the end. During execution, users have little control, making the confirm-at-end approach brittle: a single error can cascade and force a complete restart. Confirming every step avoids such failures, but imposes tedious overhead. Balancing excessive interruptions against costly rollbacks remains an open challenge. We address this problem by modeling confirmation as a minimum time scheduling problem. We conducted a formative study with eight participants, which revealed a recurring Confirmation-Diagnosis-Correction-Redo (CDCR) pattern in how users monitor errors. Based on this pattern, we developed a decision-theoretic model to determine time-efficient confirmation point placement. We then evaluated our approach using a within-subjects study where 48 participants monitored AI agents and repaired their mistakes while executing tasks. Results show that 81 percent of participants preferred our intermediate confirmation approach over the confirm-at-end approach used by existing systems, and task completion time was reduced by 13.54 percent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Proceedings of the 2026 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI '26), April 13--17, 2026, Barcelona, Spain"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:40:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huayi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongguang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MADS: Multi-Agent Dialogue Simulation for Diverse Persuasion Data Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T06:55:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T18:12:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T02:50:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:18:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T13:48:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:51:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose MADS (Multi-Agent Dialogue Simulation), a scalable framework for generating persuasive multi-turn dialogues via agent self-play. MADS employs three coordinated agents: User Agents designed to simulate diverse persona-driven behaviors by leveraging personality signifiers such as Zodiac Signs and MBTI types, a Dialog Agent executing task-oriented persuasion strategies and an Optimization Agent evaluating and refining dialogue outcomes. We further validate its effectiveness through users' Chain-of-Attitude (CoA) modeling and dedicated LLMs' persuasion assessment. This approach enables low-cost generation of training data without human annotation, addressing key industry challenges such as lack of user data, cold-start evaluation difficulties, and prompt inefficiency. Applied to a real-world marketing scenario, MADS significantly improved the persuasion capacity of small LLMs, increasing the organic traffic conversion rate by 22.4% (from 1.83% to 2.24%) , demonstrating clear business value."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Industry Track (https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-industry.26.pdf)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:38Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-10T02:28:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingrui","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Xingrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghe","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xinghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiapu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiapu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Large-Scale Chinese Knowledge Graph-Text Alignment Dataset for Benchmarking Knowledge-Grounded LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:33:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T14:06:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:56:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable evaluation of knowledge-grounded Large Language Models (LLMs) in Chinese requires resources that explicitly align Chinese-language text with verifiable Knowledge Graph (KG) facts. Yet existing Chinese benchmarks primarily assess general language understanding and offer limited support for structured reasoning under Chinese-specific linguistic phenomena. We introduce the Chinese Data-Text Pair (CDTP), a large-scale Chinese KG-text alignment dataset comprising more than 7 million aligned instances across four broad domains. Each instance pairs a Chinese-language text with one or more textually supported KG triples, totaling 15 million triples. A multi-stage construction pipeline combining alignment filtering, manual verification, and external evidence validation improves semantic consistency and factual reliability. CDTP supports Knowledge Graph Completion (KGC), Question Answering (QA), and Triple-to-Text Generation (T2T). Across all three tasks, the benchmark design accounts for Chinese-specific phenomena, including polysemy, word-segmentation ambiguity, and context-dependent entity interpretation, enabling the evaluation of structured reasoning, ambiguity-aware factual understanding, and knowledge-grounded generation. Experiments with diverse open-source and proprietary LLMs show that model scale alone does not guarantee reliable performance on these Chinese knowledge-intensive tasks, whereas supervised fine-tuning on CDTP consistently improves in-domain performance and out-of-distribution robustness. The publicly accessible dataset, code, and evaluation protocols provide a reusable resource for developing and evaluating knowledge-grounded LLMs in Chinese."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haneul","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Haneul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiho","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyunghyun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Kyunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alice","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Alice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gradual Code-Switching as Inference-Time Cross-Lingual Representational Alignment for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:35:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T12:53:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:47:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models (LLMs) have achieved notable progress in multilingual settings, their performance remains uneven across languages as LLMs often rely on English-centric latent representations. In this work, we introduce code-switching in-context learning (CSICL), an inference-time mechanism for cross-lingual representational alignment. Rather than relying on an abrupt translation pivot, CSICL explicitly scaffolds the reasoning trajectory by gradually transitioning from a target language to English, aligning non-English inputs with an English-centric reasoning space. Across 4 LLMs, 6 datasets, and 10 languages, CSICL consistently outperforms cross-lingual in-context learning baselines, yielding average gains of 6.0pp and 4.8pp in target and unseen languages, respectively. The improvements generalize across language families and are even more pronounced in low-resource settings, with gains of 14.7pp in target and 5.3pp in unseen languages. These findings establish code-switching as a robust and effective approach for reducing cross-lingual misalignment during inference, moving LLMs towards more equitable and effective multilingual systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:22:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05159","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05159","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05159","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Léo","familyName":"Boisvert","name":"Boisvert, Léo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhay","familyName":"Puri","name":"Puri, Abhay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandra Kiran Reddy","familyName":"Evuru","name":"Evuru, Chandra Kiran Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nazanin","familyName":"Sepahvand","name":"Sepahvand, Nazanin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Chapados","name":"Chapados, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quentin","familyName":"Cappart","name":"Cappart, Quentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Stanley","name":"Stanley, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Lacoste","name":"Lacoste, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishnamurthy Dj","familyName":"Dvijotham","name":"Dvijotham, Krishnamurthy Dj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Drouin","name":"Drouin, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Malice in Agentland: Down the Rabbit Hole of Backdoors in the AI Supply Chain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T12:47:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T15:35:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:58:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T19:02:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T13:35:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:53:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-03T18:11:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:03:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While finetuning AI agents on interaction data -- such as web browsing or tool use -- improves their capabilities, it also introduces critical security vulnerabilities within the agentic AI supply chain. We show that adversaries can effectively poison the data collection pipeline at multiple stages to embed hard-to-detect backdoors that, when triggered, cause unsafe or malicious behavior. We formalize three realistic threat models across distinct layers of the supply chain: direct poisoning of finetuning data, pre-backdoored base models, and environment poisoning, a novel attack vector that exploits vulnerabilities specific to agentic training pipelines. Evaluated on two widely adopted agentic benchmarks, all three threat models prove effective: poisoning only a small number of demonstrations is sufficient to embed a backdoor that causes an agent to leak confidential user information with over 80\\% success."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05159","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:26Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:29:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousef","familyName":"Emami","name":"Emami, Yousef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel Gutierrez","familyName":"Gaitan","name":"Gaitan, Miguel Gutierrez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atefeh Hajijamali","familyName":"Arani","name":"Arani, Atefeh Hajijamali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AIC-VDS: Attention-Based In-Context Learning for Joint Velocity Control and Data Collection Scheduling in Multi-UAV-Assisted Pipeline Monitoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"53-01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:04:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-03T14:54:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T02:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs) are increasingly deployed for autonomous inspection and sensor data collection in large-scale infrastructure monitoring applications, such as pipeline monitoring, where timely anomaly detection is critical. Jointly optimizing data-collection schedules and flight velocities is a critical challenge, as inefficiencies can increase packet loss and inspection latency. While online deep reinforcement learning (DRL) is a widely investigated approach, it suffers from low sample efficiency, substantial training requirements, and simulation-to-reality gaps in time-sensitive scenarios. Large language models (LLMs) offer a promising alternative through in-context learning (ICL); however, their substantial input requirements can introduce considerable computational and communication overhead. To address this, we propose Attention-Based In-Context Learning for Velocity Control and Data Collection Scheduling (AIC-VDS), a joint optimization framework designed to minimize packet loss under partial and potentially outdated local network-state information. AIC-VDS utilizes an attention module to process real-time network-state data, including sensor battery levels, sensor queue lengths, communication channel conditions, UAV locations, time since the previous sensor visit, and sensor urgency scores. This module extracts task-relevant features to reduce input overhead before querying the LLM. The LLM leverages these compressed natural-language prompts to generate adaptive data-collection schedules and velocity-control decisions for UAV execution. Simulation results show that the attention-based representation reduces the average prompt length by 50\\%, while AIC-VDS rapidly stabilizes packet loss in the considered scenario."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:36:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05592","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05592","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05592","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuofeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhuofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungju","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Seungju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwen","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jianwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In-the-Flow Agentic System Optimization for Effective Planning and Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-22T06:43:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-23T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Outcome-driven reinforcement learning has advanced reasoning in large language models (LLMs), but prevailing tool-augmented approaches train a single, monolithic policy that interleaves thoughts and tool calls under full context; this scales poorly with long horizons and diverse tools and generalizes weakly to new scenarios. Agentic systems offer a promising alternative by decomposing work across specialized modules, yet most remain training-free or rely on offline training decoupled from the live dynamics of multi-turn interaction. We introduce AgentFlow, a trainable, in-the-flow agentic framework that coordinates four modules (planner, executor, verifier, generator) through an evolving memory and directly optimizes its planner inside the multi-turn loop. To train on-policy in live environments, we propose Flow-based Group Refined Policy Optimization (Flow-GRPO), which tackles long-horizon, sparse-reward credit assignment by converting multi-turn optimization into a sequence of tractable single-turn policy updates. It broadcasts a single, verifiable trajectory-level outcome to every turn to align local planner decisions with global success and stabilizes learning with group-normalized advantages. Across ten benchmarks, AgentFlow with a 7B-scale backbone outperforms top-performing baselines with average accuracy gains of 14.9% on search, 14.0% on agentic, 14.5% on mathematical, and 4.1% on scientific tasks, even surpassing larger proprietary models like GPT-4o. Further analyses confirm the benefits of in-the-flow optimization, showing improved planning, enhanced tool-calling reliability, and positive scaling with model size and reasoning turns."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages, 12 figures. ICLR 2026 Oral. Project website: https://agentflow.stanford.edu/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05592","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-23T02:07:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunbowen","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sunbowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Qingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chak Tou","familyName":"Leong","name":"Leong, Chak Tou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jialiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing the Difficulty Perception Mechanism of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:24:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T07:13:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:55:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-19T06:24:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:50:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed on complex reasoning tasks, yet little is known about their ability to internally evaluate problem difficulty, which is an essential capability for adaptive reasoning and efficient resource allocation. In this work, we investigate whether LLMs implicitly encode problem difficulty in their internal representations. Using a linear probe on the final-token representations of LLMs, we demonstrate that the difficulty level of math problems can be linearly modeled. We further locate the specific attention heads of the final Transformer layer: these attention heads have opposite activation patterns for simple and difficult problems, thus achieving perception of difficulty. Our ablation experiments prove the accuracy of the location. Crucially, our experiments provide practical support for using LLMs as automatic difficulty annotators, potentially substantially reducing reliance on costly human labeling in benchmark construction and curriculum learning. We also uncover that there is a significant difference in entropy and difficulty perception at the token level. Our study reveals that difficulty perception in LLMs is not only present but also structurally organized, offering new theoretical insights and practical directions for future research. Our code is available at https://github.com/Aegis1863/Difficulty-Perception-of-LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:18Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petter","familyName":"Holme","name":"Holme, Petter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milena","familyName":"Tsvetkova","name":"Tsvetkova, Milena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificially intelligent agents in the social and behavioral sciences: A history and outlook"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:02:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T08:48:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:33:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-19T04:13:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:48:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We review the historical development and current trends of artificially intelligent agents (agentic AI) in the social and behavioral sciences: from the first programmable computers, and social simulations soon thereafter, to today's experiments with large language models. This overview emphasizes the role of AI in the scientific process and the changes brought about, both through technological advancements and the broader evolution of science from around 1950 to the present. Some of the specific points we cover include: the challenges of presenting the first social simulation studies to a world unaware of computers, the rise of social systems science, intelligent game theoretic agents, the age of big data and the epistemic upheaval in its wake, and the current enthusiasm around applications of generative AI, and many other topics. A pervasive theme is how deeply entwined we are with the technologies we use to understand ourselves."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Bou","name":"Bou, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nyal","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Nyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Jagota","name":"Jagota, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyapriya","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Satyapriya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonali","familyName":"Parbhoo","name":"Parbhoo, Sonali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Alignment Auditor: A Bayesian Framework for Verifying and Refining LLM Objectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:25:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T10:07:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T18:48:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:07:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The objectives that Large Language Models (LLMs) implicitly optimize remain dangerously opaque, making trustworthy alignment and auditing a grand challenge. While Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) can infer reward functions from behaviour, existing approaches either produce a single, overconfident reward estimate or fail to address the fundamental ambiguity of the task (non-identifiability). This paper introduces a principled auditing framework that re-frames reward inference from a simple estimation task to a comprehensive process for verification. Our framework leverages Bayesian IRL to not only recover a distribution over objectives but to enable three critical audit capabilities: (i) Quantifying and systematically reducing non-identifiability by demonstrating posterior contraction over sequential rounds of evidence; (ii) Providing actionable, uncertainty-aware diagnostics that expose spurious shortcuts and identify out-of-distribution prompts where the inferred objective cannot be trusted; and (iii) Validating policy-level utility by showing that the refined, low-uncertainty reward can be used directly in RLHF to achieve training dynamics and toxicity reductions comparable to the ground-truth alignment process. Empirically, our framework successfully audits a detoxified LLM, yielding a well-calibrated and interpretable objective that strengthens alignment guarantees. Overall, this work provides a practical toolkit for auditors, safety teams, and regulators to verify what LLMs are truly trying to achieve, moving us toward more trustworthy and accountable AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:17Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Riedl","name":"Riedl, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emergent Coordination in Multi-Agent Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T21:22:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-15T14:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T21:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When are multi-agent LLM systems merely a collection of individual agents versus an integrated collective with higher-order structure? We introduce an information-theoretic framework to test -- in a purely data-driven way -- whether multi-agent systems show signs of higher-order structure. This information decomposition lets us measure whether dynamical emergence is present in multi-agent LLM systems, localize it, and distinguish spurious temporal coupling from performance-relevant cross-agent synergy. We implement a practical criterion and an emergence capacity criterion operationalized as partial information decomposition of time-delayed mutual information (TDMI). We apply our framework to experiments using a simple guessing game without direct agent communication and minimal group-level feedback with three randomized interventions. Groups in the control condition exhibit strong temporal synergy but little coordinated alignment across agents. Assigning a persona to each agent introduces stable identity-linked differentiation. Combining personas with an instruction to ``think about what other agents might do'' shows identity-linked differentiation and goal-directed complementarity across agents. Taken together, our framework establishes that multi-agent LLM systems can be steered with prompt design from mere aggregates to higher-order collectives. Our results are robust across emergence measures and entropy estimators, and not explained by coordination-free baselines or temporal dynamics alone. Without attributing human-like cognition to the agents, the patterns of interaction we observe mirror well-established principles of collective intelligence in human groups: effective performance requires both alignment on shared objectives and complementary contributions across members."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theodore Jerome","familyName":"Tinker","name":"Tinker, Theodore Jerome","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenji","familyName":"Doya","name":"Doya, Kenji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Tani","name":"Tani, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curiosity-Driven Development of Action and Language in Robots Through Self-Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:53:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T02:20:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:18:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T03:27:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:21:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:36:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:12:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-16T05:35:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:24:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-03T07:36:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06-18T04:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:23:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infants acquire language with generalization from minimal experience, whereas large language models require billions of training tokens. What underlies efficient development in humans? We investigated this problem through experiments wherein robotic agents learn to perform actions associated with imperative sentences (e.g., push red cube) via curiosity-driven self-exploration. Our approach amortizes active inference using Q-learning, enabling intrinsically motivated developmental learning. The simulations reveal key findings corresponding to observations in developmental psychology. i) Generalization improves drastically as the scale of compositional elements increases. ii) Curiosity-driven exploration enables faster learning. iii) Rote pairing of sentences and actions precedes compositional generalization. iv) Exception-handling induces U-shaped developmental performance, a pattern like representational redescription in child language learning. These results suggest that curiosity-driven active inference accounts for how intrinsically motivated sensorimotor-linguistic learning supports scalable compositional generalization and exception handling in humans and artificial agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 22 pages of supplementary material"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:17:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myung Ho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Myung Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured Cognitive Loop for Behavioral Intelligence in Large Language Model Agents (Extended Revision: From Behavioral Architecture to Epistemic Accountability)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T17:43:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T05:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-12T08:18:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:31:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-28T14:49:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:31:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-17T14:35:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:57:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The central challenge for AI agents is not only performance but accountability. Agents that act through opaque prompt sequences may produce correct outputs, but they provide little basis for verifying why an action was permitted, where an error occurred, or how responsibility should be assigned. This paper presents the Structured Cognitive Loop as an architecture for accountable behavior in large language model agents. SCL separates cognition, memory, control, and action into distinct modules. The language model proposes. External memory preserves verified state. A lightweight controller checks preconditions, prevents redundant actions, and authorizes execution before tools are used. We evaluate SCL against ReAct and common LangChain agent variants across travel planning, conditional email drafting, and constraint guided image generation. Across 360 episodes, SCL achieves 86.3 percent task success compared with 70.5 to 76.8 percent for prompt based baselines. It also improves goal fidelity, reduces redundant tool calls, increases reuse of intermediate state, and lowers unsupported assertions. This extended revision situates SCL within a broader architecture of epistemic accountability. Subsequent extensions integrate context aware Human in the Loop control, Pool Gated Retrieval, and the Horizon Warrant Commitment framework. Together these components define an agent architecture in which the model proposes, structure decides, evidence is warranted before use, and human judgment is embedded in the trace rather than imposed after the fact. The result is a foundation for AI agents whose decisions are not only effective but also authorized, inspectable, and accountable."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This revised version extends the original SCL framework from a behavioral architecture for reliable LLM agents into a broader architecture of epistemic accountability, integrating context-aware Human-in-the-Loop control, Pool-Gated Retrieval, and the Horizon-Warrant-Commitment structure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:18:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05150","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05150","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05150","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Donghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuerui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xuerui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hexin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nana","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Nana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eng Siong","familyName":"Chng","name":"Chng, Eng Siong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chronological Thinking in Full-Duplex Spoken Dialogue Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T10:28:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T21:35:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:11:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T14:26:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in spoken dialogue language models (SDLMs) reflect growing interest in shifting from turn-based to full-duplex systems, where the models continuously perceive user speech streams while generating responses. This simultaneous listening and speaking design enables real-time interaction and the agent can handle dynamic conversational behaviors like user barge-in. However, during the listening phase, existing systems keep the agent idle by repeatedly predicting the silence token, which departs from human behavior: we usually engage in lightweight thinking during conversation rather than remaining absent-minded. Inspired by this, we propose Chronological Thinking, an on-the-fly conversational thinking mechanism that aims to improve response quality in full-duplex SDLMs. Specifically, chronological thinking presents a paradigm shift from conventional LLM thinking approaches, such as Chain-of-Thought, purpose-built for streaming acoustic input. (1) Strictly causal: the agent reasons incrementally while listening, updating internal hypotheses only from past audio with no lookahead. (2) No additional latency: reasoning is amortized during the listening window; once the user stops speaking, the agent halts thinking and begins speaking without further delay. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of chronological thinking through both objective metrics and human evaluations show consistent improvements in response quality. Furthermore, chronological thinking robustly handles conversational dynamics and attains competitive performance on full-duplex interaction metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by SIGDIAL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05150","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T02:03:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huayi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Huayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratik","familyName":"Chaudhari","name":"Chaudhari, Pratik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Active Semantic Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:49:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:24:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:16:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop an approach for active semantic perception, which refers to using the semantics of the scene for tasks such as exploration. We build a compact, multi-layer scene graph that can represent large, complex indoor environments at various levels of abstraction, e.g., nodes corresponding to rooms, objects, walls, windows etc., as well as fine-grained details of their geometry. We develop a procedure based on large language models (LLMs) to sample new plausible scene graphs of unobserved regions that are consistent with partial observations of the scene. We develop a procedure to compute the information gain of a potential waypoint upon this scene graph to enable sophisticated spatial reasoning: for example, of the two doors that lead out of the living room, one probably leads to the kitchen and the other to the bedroom. We evaluate our approach in realistic 3D indoor apartments in simulation and also on a Unitree Go 2 robot in the real world. Qualitative and quantitative analysis shows that our approach can pin down high-level and low-level semantic information in the environment quickly and more accurately than existing approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:43Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:19:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiquan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Haiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongfan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Gongfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xinyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinchao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MixReasoning: Switching Modes to Think"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T08:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:29:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning models enhance performance by tackling problems in a step-by-step manner, decomposing them into sub-problems and exploring long chains of thought before producing an answer. However, applying extended reasoning to every step introduces substantial redundancy, as sub-problems vary widely in difficulty and complexity: a small number of pivotal steps are genuinely challenging and decisive for the final answer, while many others only involve straightforward revisions or simple computations. Therefore, a natural idea is to endow reasoning models with the ability to adaptively respond to this variation, rather than treating all steps with the same level of elaboration. To this end, we propose MixReasoning, a framework that dynamically adjusts the depth of reasoning within a single response. The resulting chain of thought then becomes a mixture of detailed reasoning on difficult steps and concise inference on simpler ones. Experiments on GSM8K, MATH-500, and AIME show that MixReasoning shortens reasoning length and substantially improves efficiency without compromising accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:13:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxian","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiaxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Iwasawa","name":"Iwasawa, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutaka","familyName":"Matsuo","name":"Matsuo, Yutaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQA-TTRL: Self-Adaptation in Audio Question Answering with Test-Time Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-22T10:18:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-23T01:33:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T05:16:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) exhibit strong capabilities in general audio understanding but remain static after deployment, limiting their adaptability to real-world data. Since supervised fine-tuning is costly, we propose AQA-TTRL, a novel framework for audio understanding that enables on-the-fly evolution via test-time reinforcement learning using only unlabeled test data. It generates pseudo-labels via majority voting and optimizes the model through reinforcement learning. To address the noise in self-generated labels, we introduce confidence weighting to adjust training signals. Furthermore, multiple-attempt sampling mitigates advantage collapse and stabilizes training. Across MMAU, MMAR, and MMSU, AQA-TTRL achieves significant average improvements of 4.42% for Qwen2.5-Omni 7B and 11.04% for the 3B model. Notably, the adapted 3B model outperforms direct inference of the unadapted 7B model, highlighting the effectiveness of test-time adaptation in audio understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:13:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Reps","name":"Reps, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Jermaine","name":"Jermaine, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MHA-RAG: Improving Efficiency, Accuracy, and Consistency by Encoding Exemplars as Soft Prompts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:41:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T21:50:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:10:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adapting Foundation Models to new domains with limited training data is challenging and computationally expensive. While prior work has demonstrated the effectiveness of using domain-specific exemplars as in-context demonstrations, we investigate whether representing exemplars purely as text is the most efficient, effective, and stable approach. We explore an alternative: representing exemplars as soft prompts with an exemplar order invariant model architecture. To this end, we introduce Multi-Head Attention Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MHA-RAG), a framework with the number of attention heads serving as a simple hyperparameter to control soft prompt-generation across different tasks. Across multiple question-answering benchmarks and model scales, MHA-RAG achieves a 20-point performance gain over standard RAG, while cutting inference costs by a factor of 10X GFLOPs-delivering both higher accuracy and greater efficiency, invariant to exemplar order."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06063","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06063","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06063","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Austin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Austin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Varvarigos","name":"Varvarigos, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ioannis","familyName":"Panitsas","name":"Panitsas, Ioannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniela","familyName":"Fernandez","name":"Fernandez, Daniela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbiao","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jinbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Maatouk","name":"Maatouk, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leandros","familyName":"Tassiulas","name":"Tassiulas, Leandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rex","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Rex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TelecomTS: A Multi-Modal Observability Dataset for Time Series and Language Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T22:44:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T22:16:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:14:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern enterprises generate vast streams of time series metrics when monitoring complex systems, known as observability data. Unlike conventional time series from domains such as climate, observability data are zero-inflated, highly stochastic, and exhibit minimal temporal structure. Despite their importance, observability datasets remain underrepresented in public benchmarks due to proprietary restrictions and privacy concerns. Existing datasets are often anonymized and normalized, removing scale information and limiting their use for tasks such as anomaly detection, root cause analysis, and multi-modal reasoning. To address this gap, we introduce TelecomTS, a large-scale observability dataset derived from a 5G telecommunications network. TelecomTS features heterogeneous, de-anonymized covariates with explicit absolute scale information and provides a diverse suite of downstream tasks, including anomaly detection, root cause analysis, and multi-modal question-answering. Benchmarking state-of-the-art time series, language, reasoning, and multi-modal foundation models reveals that existing approaches struggle with the abrupt, noisy, and high-variance dynamics characteristic of observability data. Our experiments further underscore the importance of preserving covariates' absolute scale, emphasizing the need for foundation time series models that natively leverage scale information for practical real-world observability applications. The code is available at: https://github.com/Ali-maatouk/TelecomTS."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06063","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoav","familyName":"Gur-Arieh","name":"Gur-Arieh, Yoav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mor","familyName":"Geva","name":"Geva, Mor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atticus","familyName":"Geiger","name":"Geiger, Atticus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mixing Mechanisms: How Language Models Retrieve Bound Entities In-Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:44:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T05:00:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:31:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key component of in-context reasoning is the ability of language models (LMs) to bind entities for later retrieval. For example, an LM might represent \"Ann loves pie\" by binding \"Ann\" to \"pie\", allowing it to later retrieve \"Ann\" when asked \"Who loves pie?\" Prior research on short lists of bound entities found strong evidence that LMs implement such retrieval via a positional mechanism, where \"Ann\" is retrieved based on its position in context. In this work, we find that this mechanism generalizes poorly to more complex settings; as the number of bound entities in context increases, the positional mechanism becomes noisy and unreliable in middle positions. To compensate for this, we find that LMs supplement the positional mechanism with a lexical mechanism (retrieving \"Ann\" using its bound counterpart \"pie\") and a reflexive mechanism (retrieving \"Ann\" through a direct pointer). Through extensive experiments on nine models and ten binding tasks, we uncover a consistent pattern in how LMs mix these mechanisms to drive model behavior. We leverage these insights to develop a causal model combining all three mechanisms that estimates next token distributions with 95% agreement. Finally, we show that our model generalizes to substantially longer inputs of open-ended text interleaved with entity groups, further demonstrating the robustness of our findings in more natural settings. Overall, our study establishes a more complete picture of how LMs bind and retrieve entities in-context."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:17Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05688","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05688","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05688","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kumar Krishna","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Kumar Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Cuadron","name":"Cuadron, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis Gaspar","familyName":"Schroeder","name":"Schroeder, Luis Gaspar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matei","familyName":"Zaharia","name":"Zaharia, Matei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph E.","familyName":"Gonzalez","name":"Gonzalez, Joseph E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ion","familyName":"Stoica","name":"Stoica, Ion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"vAttention: Verified Sparse Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:46:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T12:53:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:48:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State-of-the-art sparse attention methods for reducing decoding latency fall into two main categories: approximate top-$k$ (and its extension, top-$p$) and recently introduced sampling-based estimation. However, these approaches are fundamentally limited in their ability to approximate full attention: they fail to provide consistent approximations across heads and query vectors and, most critically, lack guarantees on approximation quality, limiting their practical deployment. We observe that top-$k$ and random sampling are complementary: top-$k$ performs well when attention scores are dominated by a few tokens, whereas random sampling provides better estimates when attention scores are relatively uniform. Building on this insight and leveraging the statistical guarantees of sampling, we introduce vAttention, the first practical sparse attention mechanism with user-specified $(ε, δ)$ guarantees on approximation accuracy (thus, \"verified\"). These guarantees make vAttention a compelling step toward practical, reliable deployment of sparse attention at scale. By unifying top-$k$ and sampling, vAttention outperforms both individually, delivering a superior quality-efficiency trade-off. Our experiments show that vAttention significantly improves the quality of sparse attention (e.g., $\\sim$4.5 percentage points for Llama 3.1 8B Instruct and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B on RULER-HARD), and effectively bridges the gap between full and sparse attention (e.g., across datasets, it matches full model quality with up to 20x sparsity). We also demonstrate that it can be deployed in reasoning scenarios to achieve fast decoding without compromising model quality (e.g., vAttention achieves full model quality on AIME2024 at 10x sparsity with up to 32K token generations). Code: https://github.com/skylight-org/sparse-attention-hub. Webpage: https://sky-light.eecs.berkeley.edu."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05688","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05094","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05094","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05094","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Debevec","name":"Debevec, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VChain: Chain-of-Visual-Thought for Reasoning in Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T08:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent video generation models can produce smooth and visually appealing clips, but they often struggle to synthesize complex dynamics with a coherent chain of consequences. Accurately modeling visual outcomes and state transitions over time remains a core challenge. In contrast, large language and multimodal models (e.g., GPT-4o) exhibit strong visual state reasoning and future prediction capabilities. To bridge these strengths, we introduce VChain, a novel inference-time chain-of-visual-thought framework that injects visual reasoning signals from multimodal models into video generation. Specifically, VChain contains a dedicated pipeline that leverages large multimodal models to generate a sparse set of critical keyframes as snapshots, which are then used to guide the sparse inference-time visual-state adaptation of a pre-trained video generator only at these key moments. Our approach is tuning-efficient, introduces minimal overhead and avoids dense supervision. Extensive experiments on complex, multi-step scenarios show that VChain significantly enhances the quality of generated videos."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 (Findings Paper), ICCV 2025 Workshop Outstanding Paper Award, Project page: https://eyeline-labs.github.io/VChain"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05094","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:06:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:06:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:29:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04905","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04905","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04905","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuante","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuante","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yepang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yepang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation: A Survey with Focus on Repository-Level Approaches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:20:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-25T16:58:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:56:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T17:52:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-21T01:16:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have significantly improved automated code generation. While existing approaches have achieved strong performance at the function and file levels, real-world software engineering requires reasoning over entire repositories, including cross-file dependencies, evolving execution environments, and global semantic consistency. This challenge has led to the emergence of Repository-Level Code Generation (RLCG), where models must retrieve, organize, and utilize repository-scale context to generate coherent and executable code changes. To address these challenges, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has become an increasingly important paradigm for repository-level code intelligence. In this survey, we present a comprehensive review of Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation (RACG), with a particular focus on repository-level approaches. Rather than viewing RACG as a static ``retrieve-then-generate'' pipeline, we characterize it as a coupled and evolving process involving context construction, retrieval optimization, generation, and environment interaction. We organize existing methods through a unified analytical framework spanning retrieval substrate, control regime, and evaluation setting. Based on this framework, we systematically examine retrieval strategies, graph-based and non-graph-based retrieval paradigms, training-driven optimizations, and autonomous agent architectures. We further summarize widely used datasets, benchmarks, and system configurations, and discuss key challenges including scalability, reliability, efficiency, and the necessity boundary between RACG and long-context LLMs. Through this survey, we aim to provide a structured understanding of the rapidly evolving RACG landscape and highlight promising directions for future AI-powered software engineering research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04905","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:58:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:58:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:35:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva Krishna Reddy","familyName":"Malay","name":"Malay, Shiva Krishna Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARM: Discovering Agentic Reasoning Modules for Generalizable Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:04:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T06:43:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:38:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-powered Multi-agent systems (MAS) have achieved state-of-the-art results on various complex reasoning tasks. Recent works have proposed techniques to automate the design of MASes, eliminating the need for manual engineering. However, these techniques perform poorly, often achieving similar or inferior performance to simple baselines. Furthermore, they require computationally expensive re-discovery of architectures for each new task domain and expensive data annotation on domains without existing labeled validation sets. A critical insight is that simple Chain of Thought (CoT) reasoning often performs competitively with these complex systems, suggesting that the fundamental reasoning unit of MASes, CoT, warrants further investigation. To this end, we present a new paradigm for automatic MAS design that pivots the focus to optimizing CoT reasoning. We introduce the Agentic Reasoning Module (ARM), an agentic generalization of CoT where each granular reasoning step is executed by a specialized reasoning module. This module is discovered through a tree search over the code space, starting from a simple CoT module and evolved using mutations informed by reflection on execution traces. The resulting ARM acts as a versatile reasoning building block which can be utilized as a direct recursive loop or as a subroutine in a learned meta-orchestrator. Our approach significantly outperforms both manually designed MASes and state-of-the-art automatic MAS design methods. Crucially, MASes built with ARM exhibit superb generalization, maintaining high performance across different foundation models and task domains without further optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:46:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsien-Chin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hsien-Chin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin Matthias","familyName":"Ruppik","name":"Ruppik, Benjamin Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carel","familyName":"van Niekerk","name":"van Niekerk, Carel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Hao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chia-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Heck","name":"Heck, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurul","familyName":"Lubis","name":"Lubis, Nurul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Vukovic","name":"Vukovic, Renato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milica","familyName":"Gašić","name":"Gašić, Milica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt reinforcing for long-term planning of large language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:48:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T10:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:44:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-17T12:12:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success in a wide range of natural language processing tasks and can be adapted through prompting. However, they remain suboptimal in multi-turn interactions, often relying on incorrect early assumptions and failing to track user goals over time, which makes such tasks particularly challenging. Prior works in dialogue systems have shown that long-term planning is essential for handling interactive tasks. In this work, we propose a prompt optimisation framework inspired by reinforcement learning, which enables such planning to take place by only modifying the task instruction prompt of the LLM-based agent. By generating turn-by-turn feedback and leveraging experience replay for prompt rewriting, our proposed method shows significant improvement in multi-turn tasks such as text-to-SQL and task-oriented dialogue. Moreover, it generalises across different LLM-based agents and can leverage diverse LLMs as meta-prompting agents. This warrants future research in reinforcement learning-inspired parameter-free optimisation methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06062","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06062","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06062","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiakang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runze","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Runze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingpeng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Qingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenping","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wenping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuzheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guorui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Gai","name":"Gai, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Importance Sampling Misallocates Credit: Asymmetric Ratios for Outcome-Supervised RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-15T05:25:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:27:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has shown great promise in large language models (LLMs) post-training, which typically rely on token-level clipping to maintain stability during optimization. Despite the empirical success of GRPO-style methods, we identify a fundamental and previously overlooked challenge in this popular Outcome-Supervised RL (OSRL) paradigm. We reveal that in OSRL, where advantages are shared across tokens within a response, importance sampling (IS) ratios deviate from their traditional purpose of distribution correction as in classic RL, which become token-level weights that allocate the shared advantage signal across tokens. We show that this hidden role shift induces a critical mismatch for positive-advantage tokens, leading to unbalanced token weighting between positive and negative tokens. Specifically, it suppresses the update of underrepresented tokens that are lagging behind, while over-amplifying already high-probability tokens. This mismatch results in rich-get-richer dynamics that over-reinforce confident tokens, weaken catch-up learning that drive entropy collapse, excessive repetition, and premature convergence. To address this, we propose Asymmetric Importance Sampling Policy Optimization (ASPO), a simple yet effective strategy that reverses the ratio-induced weighting of positive-advantage tokens, while stabilizing extreme updates and maintaining gradient flow. This mismatch correction aligns their update direction with the learning dynamics of negative ones. Comprehensive experiments across math reasoning and coding benchmarks demonstrate that ASPO significantly mitigates entropy collapse, improves training stability, and enhances performance over strong GRPO-based baselines. Our analysis provides new insights into the role of token-level weighting in OSRL and highlights the critical importance of correcting ratio-induced weighting in LLM RL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06062","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:30Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-18T02:13:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeong Kyu","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Hyeong Kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting Distillation Data from Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:37:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:23:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T06:36:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning distillation has emerged as a prevailing paradigm for transferring reasoning capabilities from large reasoning models to small language models. Yet, reasoning distillation risks data contamination: benchmark data may inadvertently be included in the distillation data, thereby inflating model performance metrics. In this work, we formally define the distillation data detection task, which determines whether a given question is included in the model's distillation data. The unique challenge of this task lies in the partial availability of distillation data. To address this, we propose Token Probability Deviation (TPD), a detection method that leverages the probability patterns of output tokens generated by the model instead of input tokens. Our method is motivated by the observation that seen questions tend to elicit more near-deterministic tokens generated by the models than unseen ones. Our TPD score is thus designed to quantify the token-level deviation of generated tokens from a high-confidence reference probability. Consequently, seen questions can yield substantially lower TPD scores than unseen ones, enabling strong detection performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, improving detection AUC by up to 31% on distillation datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:55:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:55:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-11T02:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songyuan","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Songyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training-Free Time Series Classification via In-Context Reasoning with LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:07:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:50:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-30T21:41:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T00:11:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series classification (TSC) spans diverse application scenarios, yet labeled data are often scarce, making task-specific training costly and inflexible. Recent reasoning-oriented large language models (LLMs) show promise in understanding temporal patterns, but purely zero-shot usage remains suboptimal. We propose FETA, a multi-agent framework for training-free TSC via exemplar-based in-context reasoning. FETA decomposes a multivariate series into channel-wise subproblems, retrieves a few structurally similar labeled examples for each channel, and leverages a reasoning LLM to compare the query against these exemplars, producing channel-level labels with self-assessed confidences; a confidence-weighted aggregator then fuses all channel decisions. This design eliminates the need for pretraining or fine-tuning, improves efficiency by pruning irrelevant channels and controlling input length, and enhances interpretability through exemplar grounding and confidence estimation. On nine challenging UEA datasets, FETA achieves strong accuracy under a fully training-free setting, surpassing multiple trained baselines. These results demonstrate that a multi-agent in-context reasoning framework can transform LLMs into competitive, plug-and-play TSC solvers without any parameter training. The code is available at https://github.com/SongyuanSui/FETATSC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages main content, 12 pages total including appendix, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-04T01:54:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca","familyName":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Francesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From surveillance to signalling: escalation channels as environmental controls for agentic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:37:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T21:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:11:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When AI agents operating with access to sensitive information encounter a conflict between completing an assigned task and following rules or ethical constraints, they can resort to unsanctioned behaviour. Existing inference time safety work addresses this primarily through monitoring and access restriction. We investigate a complementary and under-explored layer: environmental controls that act on the agent's decision context at the point of conflict, making it more likely that the agent takes an authorised alternative path rather than an unsanctioned one. Drawing on Situational Crime Prevention (SCP), a framework used in human insider risk management to make harmful actions less rewarding and compliant actions more viable by design choices in the environment, we design and evaluate escalation channels as a concrete instantiation of this control class. An escalation channel provides an agent with a formal, out-of-band route to surface a conflict to an independent authority. We evaluate two designs: a simple email escalation and an instrumentally credible channel that guarantees a 30-minute pause and independent review, making the authorised path genuinely useful for goal achievement rather than merely nominally available. Across 10 frontier LLMs using the agentic task-rule conflict scenario of Lynch et al. (2025), we find that without any control the harmful action rate is 38.73%. A simple escalation channel reduces this to 5.92%; the instrumentally credible channel reduces it further to 1.21%, a statistically significant improvement observed in all 10 models tested across 24,000 samples. Our results suggest that the instrumental credibility of the authorised alternative matters considerably, and that environmental control design is a productive and largely unexplored addition to the defence-in-depth toolkit for agentic AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:32:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry Jingchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Terry Jingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinya","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zirong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueling","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yueling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhe","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ruizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijing","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youpeng","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Youpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingqian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Bingqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhua","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jianhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aligning Perception, Reasoning, Modeling and Interaction: A Survey on Physical AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:16:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T08:00:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:39:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-19T02:43:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:43:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-22T12:44:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-23T02:21:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-30T05:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:25:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of embodied intelligence and world models has intensified efforts to integrate physical laws into AI systems, yet physical perception and symbolic physics reasoning have developed along separate trajectories without a unified bridging framework. This work provides a comprehensive overview of physical AI, establishing clear distinctions between theoretical physics reasoning and applied physical understanding while systematically examining how physics-grounded methods enhance AI's real-world comprehension across structured symbolic reasoning, embodied systems, and generative models. Through rigorous analysis of recent advances, we advocate for intelligent systems that ground learning in both physical principles and embodied reasoning processes, transcending pattern recognition toward genuine understanding of physical laws. Our synthesis envisions next-generation world models capable of explaining physical phenomena and predicting future states, advancing safe, generalizable, and interpretable AI systems. We maintain a continuously updated resource at https://github.com/AI4Phys/Awesome-AI-for-Physics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:32:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06002","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06002","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06002","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hudson","familyName":"de Martim","name":"de Martim, Hudson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deterministic Legal Agents: A Canonical Primitive API for Auditable Reasoning over Temporal Knowledge Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:04:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T16:44:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:56:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T19:28:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:05:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In high-stakes legal domains, retrieval must preserve not only semantic relevance, but also the hierarchy, temporality, and causal provenance of legal norms. Standard Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), based mainly on semantic similarity over text fragments, cannot reliably provide this level of control. Prior work on SAT-Graph RAG addressed the representation problem by modeling legal materials as structure-aware temporal knowledge graphs. This paper addresses the next problem: how an LLM-based reasoning agent can interact with such a graph without reintroducing unreliable retrieval behavior.\n We specify the SAT-Graph API, a canonical primitive interface for auditable reasoning over temporal knowledge graphs, developed and illustrated in the legal domain. The API exposes typed, atomic, and composable primitives that mediate between a probabilistic language model and a deterministic symbolic substrate. Its design follows Probability Isolation: uncertainty is confined to intent translation, semantic anchoring, and final narrative synthesis, while structural, temporal, and causal graph traversals are executed through deterministic operations over canonical anchors.\n The interface shifts legal RAG from single-shot Retrieve-then-Generate to active Reason-Act-Observe. An agent decomposes a legal question into an explicit execution plan, invokes primitives for point-in-time retrieval, context reconstruction, provenance tracing, and impact analysis, and produces an answer grounded in an auditable log of graph operations. The result is a formal architectural specification, not an empirical benchmark: a secure interaction protocol that decouples legal knowledge representation from agentic reasoning. Although illustrated in law, the primitive model is domain-portable to other temporally versioned, provenance-sensitive, and authority-governed knowledge bases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Substantially revised version consolidating the paper as a formal SAT-Graph API specification: clarifies Probability Isolation and post-anchoring determinism, broadens semantic anchoring to open and thematic legal queries, refines the data models and temporal primitives, and strengthens the use cases, limitations, and bibliography"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06002","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05432","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05432","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05432","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shambhavi","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Shambhavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurav","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Gaurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pedersoli","name":"Pedersoli, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laurent","familyName":"Charlin","name":"Charlin, Laurent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Dolz","name":"Dolz, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Pal","name":"Pal, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AInstein: Can LLMs Solve Research Problems From Parametric Memory Alone?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:50:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-28T12:36:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:49:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Can large language models solve AI research problems using only their parametric knowledge, without fine-tuning, retrieval, or other external aids? We introduce AInstein, a framework for testing whether LLM agents can generate and refine solutions to research problems through iterative critique loops. A blind study with 20 domain experts on held-out ICLR 2026 problems validates our automated metrics, which we then scale to 1,214 ICLR 2025 papers using an LLM-as-a-judge paradigm. Two metrics capture complementary aspects of performance: Success Rate (does the solution address the problem?) and Rediscovery (does it match the published approach?). LLMs succeed on over 70% of problems, yet strictly rediscover the published solution less than 19% of the time, suggesting genuine problem-solving rather than associative recall. However, this ability has clear limits: models handle familiar methodological territory well but fail when solutions require cross-domain analogical transfer, a pattern we call the parametric knowledge boundary. On the ResearchPlanGen benchmark (2,645 problems), our training-free iterative refinement strategy matches RL finetuning, and a criteria-coverage analysis pins down the ceiling of what test-time refinement alone can achieve. Together, these findings map both the capabilities and the limits of LLMs as autonomous scientific problem-solvers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05432","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-29T02:32:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzheng","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Shuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangyang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Kangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanchao","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Fanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Minjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baobao","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Baobao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Maosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T03:25:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:36:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T04:33:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:32:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents based on large language models (LLMs) struggle with brainless trial-and-error and generating hallucinatory actions due to a lack of global planning in long-horizon tasks. In this paper, we introduce a plan-and-execute framework and propose EAGLET, an efficient and effective planner training method to enhance the executor agent's planning abilities without human effort. Specifically, we train a plug-and-play global planner through a two-step process: we first synthesize high-quality plans from an advanced LLM using our proposed homologous consensus filtering strategy, and apply fine-tuning as a cold start. Moreover, we further improve the planner with a rule-based reinforcement learning stage using a novel executor capability gain reward, ensuring it can handle task instructions of varying difficulty. Experiments on three long-horizon agent tasks show that executor agents equipped with our planner outperform existing methods, achieving new state-of-the-art performance. Meanwhile, EAGLET reduces training costs by 8x compared to RL-based baselines, and it does not require manual effort or extra training data, offering an efficient and effective solution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenda","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Wenda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqing","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Yanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junle","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisheng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Yisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei-Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fei-Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Plug-and-Play Dramaturge: A Divide-and-Conquer Approach for Iterative Narrative Script Refinement via Collaborative LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:20:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T08:29:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:29:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T04:52:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:06:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLMs have been widely adopted for creative content generation, a single-pass process often struggles to produce high-quality long narratives. How to effectively revise and improve long narrative scripts like scriptwriters remains a significant challenge, as it demands a comprehensive understanding of the entire context to identify global structural issues and local detailed flaws, as well as coordinating revisions at multiple granularities and locations. Direct modifications by LLMs typically introduce inconsistencies between local edits and the overall narrative requirements. To address these issues, we propose Dramaturge, a task and feature oriented divide-and-conquer approach powered by hierarchical multiple LLM agents. It consists of a Global Review stage to grasp the overall storyline and structural issues, a Scene-level Review stage to pinpoint detailed scene and sentence flaws, and a Hierarchical Coordinated Revision stage that coordinates and integrates structural and detailed improvements throughout the script. The top-down task flow ensures that high-level strategies guide local modifications, maintaining contextual consistency. The review and revision workflow follows a coarse-to-fine iterative process, continuing through multiple rounds until no further substantive improvements can be made. Comprehensive experiments show that Dramaturge significantly outperforms all baselines in terms of script-level overall quality and scene-level details. Our approach is plug-and-play and can be easily integrated into existing methods to improve the generated scripts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yongan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianda","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xianda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingchen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Qiang","name":"Qiang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grant","familyName":"McKenzie","name":"McKenzie, Grant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renee","familyName":"Sieber","name":"Sieber, Renee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengran","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Fengran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeatherArchive-Bench: Benchmarking Retrieval-Augmented Reasoning for Historical Weather Archives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-19T18:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:22:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Historical archives on weather events are collections of enduring primary source records that offer rich, untapped narratives of how societies have experienced and responded to extreme weather events. These qualitative accounts provide insights into societal vulnerability and resilience that are largely absent from meteorological records, making them valuable for climate scientists to understand societal responses. However, their vast scale, noisy digitized quality, and archaic language make it difficult to transform them into structured knowledge for climate research. To address this challenge, we introduce WeatherArchive-Bench, the first benchmark for evaluating retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems on historical weather archives. WeatherArchive-Bench comprises two tasks: WeatherArchive-Retrieval, which measures a system's ability to locate historically relevant passages from over one million archival news segments, and WeatherArchive-Assessment, which evaluates whether Large Language Models (LLMs) can classify societal vulnerability and resilience indicators from extreme weather narratives. Extensive experiments across sparse, dense, and re-ranking retrievers, as well as a diverse set of LLMs, reveal that dense retrievers often fail on historical terminology, while LLMs frequently misinterpret vulnerability and resilience concepts. These findings highlight key limitations in reasoning about complex societal indicators and provide insights for designing more robust climate-focused RAG systems from archival contexts. The constructed dataset and evaluation framework are publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/WeatherArchive-Bench/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to the Resource Track of SIGIR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05486","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05486","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05486","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cassie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Cassie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuti","familyName":"Mohan","name":"Mohan, Stuti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefanie","familyName":"Tellex","name":"Tellex, Stefanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T19:23:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:07:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs have been widely used in planning, either as planners to generate action sequences end-to-end, or as formalizers to represent the planning domain and problem in a formal language that can derive plans deterministically. However, both lines of work rely on standard benchmarks that include only generic and simplistic environmental specifications, leading to potential overestimation of the planning ability of LLMs and safety concerns in downstream tasks. We bridge this gap by augmenting widely used planning benchmarks with manually annotated, fine-grained, and rich natural language constraints spanning four formally defined categories. Over 4 state-of-the-art reasoning LLMs, 4 formal languages, and 4 datasets, we show that the introduction of one-sentence constraints consistently halves performance, indicating current LLMs' lack of robustness and an avenue for future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In ACL 2026 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05486","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:18:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexis","familyName":"Ross","name":"Ross, Alexis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Megha","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Megha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremiah","familyName":"Blanchard","name":"Blanchard, Jeremiah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Andreas","name":"Andreas, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling Student Learning with 3.8 Million Program Traces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T07:25:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:33:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As programmers write code, they often edit and retry multiple times, creating rich \"interaction traces\" that reveal how they approach coding tasks and provide clues about their level of skill development. For novice programmers in particular, these traces reflect the diverse reasoning processes they employ to code, such as exploratory behavior to understand how a programming concept works, re-strategizing in response to bugs, and personalizing stylistic choices. In this work, we explore what can be learned from training language models on such reasoning traces: not just about code, but about coders, and particularly students learning to program. We introduce a dataset of over 3.8 million programming reasoning traces from users of Pencil Code, a free online educational platform used by students to learn simple programming concepts. Compared to models trained only on final programs or synthetically-generated traces, we find that models trained on real traces are stronger at modeling diverse student behavior. Through both behavioral and probing analyses, we also find that many properties of code traces, such as goal backtracking or number of comments, can be predicted from learned representations of the students who write them. Building on this result, we show that we can help students recover from mistakes by steering code generation models to identify a sequence of edits that will results in more correct code while remaining close to the original student's style. Together, our results suggest that many properties of code are properties of individual students and that training on edit traces can lead to models that are more steerable, more predictive of student behavior while programming, and better at generating programs in their final states. Code and data is available at https://github.com/meghabyte/pencilcode-public"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 27th International Conference on AI in Education (AIED 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05837","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05837","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05837","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueting","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xueting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hinrich","familyName":"Schutze","name":"Schutze, Hinrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kam-Fai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Kam-Fai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EEPO: Exploration-Enhanced Policy Optimization via Sample-Then-Forget"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:02:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-13T12:17:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:47:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Balancing exploration and exploitation remains a central challenge in reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) for large language models (LLMs). Current RLVR methods often overemphasize exploitation, leading to entropy collapse, diminished exploratory capacity, and ultimately limited performance gains. Although techniques that increase policy stochasticity can promote exploration, they frequently fail to escape dominant behavioral modes. This creates a self-reinforcing loop -- repeatedly sampling and rewarding dominant modes -- that further erodes exploration. We introduce Exploration-Enhanced Policy Optimization (EEPO), a framework that promotes exploration via two-stage rollouts with adaptive unlearning. In the first stage, the model generates half of the trajectories; it then undergoes a lightweight unlearning step to temporarily suppress these sampled responses, forcing the second stage to explore different regions of the output space. This sample-then-forget mechanism disrupts the self-reinforcing loop and promotes wider exploration during rollouts. Across five reasoning benchmarks, EEPO outperforms GRPO, achieving average relative gains of 24.3% on Qwen2.5-3B, 33.0% on Llama3.2-3B-Instruct, and 10.4% on Qwen3-8B-Base."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05837","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Shirkavand","name":"Shirkavand, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xiaokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michelle","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Michelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Catalog-Native LLM: Speaking Item-ID Dialect with Less Entanglement for Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T15:30:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T16:35:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While collaborative filtering delivers predictive accuracy and efficiency, and Large Language Models (LLMs) enable expressive and generalizable reasoning, modern recommendation systems must bring these strengths together. Growing user expectations, such as natural-language queries and transparent explanations, further highlight the need for a unified approach. However, doing so is nontrivial. Collaborative signals are often token-efficient but semantically opaque, while LLMs are semantically rich but struggle to model implicit user preferences when trained only on textual inputs. This paper introduces Item-ID + Oral-language Mixture-of-Experts Language Model (IDIOMoE), which treats item interaction histories as a native dialect within the language space, enabling collaborative signals to be understood in the same way as natural language. By splitting the Feed Forward Network of each block of a pretrained LLM into a separate text expert and an item expert with token-type gating, our method avoids destructive interference between text and catalog modalities. IDIOMoE demonstrates strong recommendation performance across both public and proprietary datasets, while preserving the text understanding of the pretrained model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuangshi","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Kuangshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan A.","familyName":"Karr","name":"Karr, Jonathan A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitesh V.","familyName":"Chawla","name":"Chawla, Nitesh V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoli","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KEO: Knowledge Extraction on OMIn via Knowledge Graphs and RAG for Safety-Critical Aviation Maintenance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:29:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-08T21:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Knowledge Extraction on OMIn (KEO), a domain-specific knowledge extraction and reasoning framework with large language models (LLMs) in safety-critical contexts. Using the Operations and Maintenance Intelligence (OMIn) dataset, we construct a QA benchmark spanning global sensemaking and actionable maintenance tasks. KEO builds a structured Knowledge Graph (KG) and integrates it into a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline, enabling more coherent, dataset-wide reasoning than traditional text-chunk RAG. We evaluate locally deployable LLMs (Gemma-3, Phi-4, Mistral-Nemo) and employ stronger models (GPT-4o, Llama-3.3) as judges. Experiments show that KEO markedly improves global sensemaking by revealing patterns and system-level insights, while text-chunk RAG remains effective for fine-grained procedural tasks requiring localized retrieval. These findings underscore the promise of KG-augmented LLMs for secure, domain-specific QA and their potential in high-stakes reasoning. The code is available at https://github.com/JonathanKarr33/keo."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgio","familyName":"Giannone","name":"Giannone, Giorgio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Guangxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil Shivakumar","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Nikhil Shivakumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohan Mahesh","familyName":"Awhad","name":"Awhad, Rohan Mahesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivchander","familyName":"Sudalairaj","name":"Sudalairaj, Shivchander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akash","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Akash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Premature Exploitation in Particle-based Monte Carlo for Inference-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:48:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T20:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:10:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference-Time Scaling (ITS) improves language models by allocating more computation at generation time. Particle Filtering (PF) has emerged as a strong ITS method for complex mathematical reasoning tasks, but it is vulnerable when guided by process reward models, which often assign overconfident scores early in the reasoning process. This causes PF to suffer from premature exploitation: it myopically commits to locally promising trajectories, prunes potentially correct hypotheses, and converges to suboptimal solutions. This failure mode, known as particle impoverishment, is especially severe under constrained computational budgets. To address this, we analyze the problem and identify two root causes: a lack of diversity in the particle set due to overconfident resampling and consequent inability to assess the potential of a reasoning path. We introduce Entropic Particle Filtering (ePF), an algorithm that integrates two new techniques to solve these issues. The first technique, Entropic Annealing (EA), directly mitigates particle impoverishment by monitoring search diversity via entropy; when diversity drops, it intervenes by dynamically annealing the resampling distribution to preserve exploration. The second, an enhancement called Look-ahead Modulation (LaM), adds a predictive guide to evaluate a state's potential based on its successors. On several challenging math benchmarks, ePF significantly outperforms strong baselines and achieves up to a 50% relative improvement in task reward. Together, these methods improve PF's resilience by balancing the exploration of diverse solution spaces with the exploitation of high-reward regions, ultimately leading to higher-quality solutions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lunyiu","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Lunyiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nedim","familyName":"Lipka","name":"Lipka, Nedim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan A.","familyName":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Ryan A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swarat","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Swarat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Tree-Structured Deep Research with Adaptive Resource Allocation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T00:15:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-29T04:59:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:50:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep research agents, which synthesize information across diverse sources, are significantly constrained by the sequential nature of reasoning. This bottleneck results in high latency, poor runtime adaptability, and inefficient resource allocation, making today's deep research systems impractical for interactive applications. To overcome this, we introduce ParallelResearch, a novel framework for efficient deep research that transforms sequential processing into parallel, runtime orchestration by dynamically decomposing complex queries into tree-structured sub-tasks.\n Our core contributions are threefold: (1) an adaptive planner that dynamically allocates computational resources based on query complexity; (2) a runtime orchestration layer that prunes redundant paths to reallocate resources and enables speculative execution; and (3) a fully-asynchronous execution infrastructure that enables concurrency across both research breadth and depth. Experiments on two benchmarks show up to 5x speedups with comparable final report quality, and consistent quality improvements with the same time budgets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Workshop on Agents in the Wild (Spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishika","familyName":"Bhagwatkar","name":"Bhagwatkar, Rishika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Kasa","name":"Kasa, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhay","familyName":"Puri","name":"Puri, Abhay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Rish","name":"Rish, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Graham W.","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Graham W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishnamurthy Dj","familyName":"Dvijotham","name":"Dvijotham, Krishnamurthy Dj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Lacoste","name":"Lacoste, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Indirect Prompt Injections: Are Firewalls All You Need, or Stronger Benchmarks?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:09:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T12:25:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:52:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are vulnerable to indirect prompt injection attacks, where malicious instructions embedded in external content or tool outputs cause unintended or harmful behavior. Inspired by the well-established concept of firewalls, we show that a simple, modular, and model-agnostic defense operating at the agent--tool interface achieves perfect security with high utility across all four public benchmarks: AgentDojo, Agent Security Bench, InjecAgent and tau-Bench, while achieving a state-of-the-art security--utility tradeoff compared to prior results. Specifically, we employ two firewalls: a Tool-Input Firewall (Minimizer) and a Tool-Output Firewall (Sanitizer). Unlike prior complex approaches, this defense makes minimal assumptions about the agent and can be deployed out of the box. This makes it highly generalizable while maintaining strong performance without compromising utility. Our analysis also reveals critical limitations in these existing benchmarks, including flawed success metrics, implementation bugs, and most importantly, weak attacks, hindering progress. To address this, we present targeted fixes to these issues for AgentDojo and Agent Security Bench, and propose best practices for more robust benchmark design. Moreover, we introduce a three-stage attack strategy that cascades standard prompt injection attacks, second-order attacks, and adaptive attacks to evaluate the robustness beyond existing attacks. Overall, our work shows that existing agentic security benchmarks are easily saturated by a simple approach and highlights the need for stronger benchmarks with carefully chosen evaluation metrics and strong adaptive attacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:26Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Dimino","name":"Dimino, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Arun","name":"Arun, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhaskarjit","familyName":"Sarmah","name":"Sarmah, Bhaskarjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Pasquali","name":"Pasquali, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinReflectKG -- EvalBench: Benchmarking Financial KG with Multi-Dimensional Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:35:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T20:05:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T00:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being used to extract structured knowledge from unstructured financial text. Although prior studies have explored various extraction methods, there is no universal benchmark or unified evaluation framework for the construction of financial knowledge graphs (KG). We introduce FinReflectKG - EvalBench, a benchmark and evaluation framework for KG extraction from SEC 10-K filings. Building on the agentic and holistic evaluation principles of FinReflectKG - a financial KG linking audited triples to source chunks from S\u0026amp;P 100 filings and supporting single-pass, multi-pass, and reflection-agent-based extraction modes - EvalBench implements a deterministic commit-then-justify judging protocol with explicit bias controls, mitigating position effects, leniency, verbosity and world-knowledge reliance. Each candidate triple is evaluated with binary judgments of faithfulness, precision, and relevance, while comprehensiveness is assessed on a three-level ordinal scale (good, partial, bad) at the chunk level. Our findings suggest that, when equipped with explicit bias controls, LLM-as-Judge protocols provide a reliable and cost-efficient alternative to human annotation, while also enabling structured error analysis. Reflection-based extraction emerges as the superior approach, achieving best performance in comprehensiveness, precision, and relevance, while single-pass extraction maintains the highest faithfulness. By aggregating these complementary dimensions, FinReflectKG - EvalBench enables fine-grained benchmarking and bias-aware evaluation, advancing transparency and governance in financial AI applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:14:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05138","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05138","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05138","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory Hok Tjoan","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Gregory Hok Tjoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khang","familyName":"Ly","name":"Ly, Khang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Tabatabaei","name":"Tabatabaei, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"de Rijke","name":"de Rijke, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiRA: A Multi-Agent Framework for Reliable and Readable Literature Review Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:14:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-25T12:22:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:49:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T13:35:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-20T15:51:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-23T01:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of scientific publications has made it increasingly difficult to keep literature reviews comprehensive and up-to-date. Though prior work has focused on automating retrieval and screening, the writing phase of systematic reviews remains largely under-explored, especially with regard to readability and factual accuracy. To address this, we present LiRA (Literature Review Agents), a multi-agent collaborative workflow which emulates the human literature review process. LiRA utilizes specialized agents for content outlining, subsection writing, editing, and reviewing, producing cohesive and comprehensive review articles. Evaluated on SciReviewGen and a proprietary ScienceDirect dataset, LiRA outperforms current baselines such as AutoSurvey and MASS-Survey in writing and citation quality, while maintaining competitive similarity to human-written reviews. We further evaluate LiRA in real-world scenarios using document retrieval and assess its robustness to reviewer model variation. Our findings highlight the potential of agentic LLM workflows, even without domain-specific tuning, to improve the reliability and usability of automated scientific writing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the 40th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence. Please cite the published version here: https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/41489"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05138","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:13:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04826","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04826","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04826","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfeng","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengru","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhengru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient structure-preserving scheme for chemotaxis PDE system with singular sensitivity in crime and epidemic modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35K61, 65M06, 65M15, 82C26, 92C17","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:09:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:35:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T04:44:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:50:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The chemotaxis PDE system with singular sensitivity was originally proposed by Short et al. (Math. Mod. Meth. Appl. Sci., 2008) as the continuum limit of a biased random walk model to account for the formation of crime hotspots and environmental feedback successfully. Recently, this idea has also been applied to epidemiology to model the impact of human social behaviors on disease transmission. In order to characterize the phase transition, pattern formation and statistical properties in the long-term dynamics, a stable and accurate numerical scheme is urgently demanded, which still remains challenging due to the positivity constraint on the singular sensitivity and the absence of an energy functional. In particular, the loss of positivity may produce nonphysical states and even cause spurious blow-up. To address these numerical challenges, this paper constructs an efficient positivity-preserving, implicit-explicit scheme with second-order accuracy. A rigorous error estimation is provided with the Lagrange multiplier correction to deal with the singular sensitivity. The whole framework is extended to a multi-agent epidemic model with degenerate diffusion, in which both positivity and mass conservation are achieved. Numerical experiments are performed to validate the theoretical results and demonstrate the necessity of the correction strategy. Our simulations reveal rich dynamical behaviors, including the phase transition between aggregation-dominated and dissipative regimes, as well as the nucleation, spread, and dissipation of crime hotspots. For the epidemic model, the results further show that spatial clustering of population density may accelerate virus transmission and significantly amplify the infectious wave."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04826","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:54:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:54:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05748","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05748","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05748","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hachem","familyName":"Madmoun","name":"Madmoun, Hachem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salem","familyName":"Lahlou","name":"Lahlou, Salem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Communication Enables Cooperation in LLM Agents: A Comparison with Curriculum-Based Approaches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:06:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T09:52:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:28:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T12:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Eliciting cooperation in multi-agent LLM systems is critical for AI alignment. We investigate two approaches: direct communication and curriculum learning. In a 4-player Stag Hunt, a one-word \"cheap talk\" channel increases cooperation from 0% to 96.7%, demonstrating communication as a robust coordination mechanism. In contrast, we find that curriculum learning is highly sensitive to design choices: our pedagogical curriculum through progressively complex games reduced agent payoffs by 27.4% in an Iterated Public Goods Game with Punishment, demonstrating that optimizing for short-term rationality can actively undermine alignment goals. Qualitative analysis reveals that curricula emphasizing defection-equilibrium games can induce \"learned pessimism\" in agents. These findings suggest that for coordination problems, simple communication protocols may be more reliable than experience-based training, and that curriculum design for social dilemmas requires careful attention to the strategic lessons embedded in game sequences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in EACL 2026 - Corrected cooperation rates for two-stage communication conditions (96.7% and 100.0%, previously reported as 48.3% and 50.0% due to a denominator bug in the evaluation code). All other results unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05748","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songhao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Songhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxiang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Boxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayak","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Sayak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Factuality Matters: When Image Generation and Editing Meet Structured Visuals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:56:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T05:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:32:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While modern visual generation models excel at creating aesthetically pleasing natural images, they struggle with producing or editing structured visuals like charts, diagrams, and mathematical figures, which demand composition planning, text rendering, and multimodal reasoning for factual fidelity. To address this, we present the first comprehensive, systematic investigation of this domain, encompassing data construction, model training, and an evaluation benchmark. First, we construct a large-scale dataset of 1.3 million high-quality structured image pairs derived from executable drawing programs and augmented with chain-of-thought reasoning annotations. Building on it, we train a unified model that integrates a VLM with FLUX.1 Kontext via a lightweight connector for enhanced multimodal understanding. A three-stage training curriculum enables progressive feature alignment, knowledge infusion, and reasoning-augmented generation, further boosted by an external reasoner at inference time. Finally, we introduce StructBench, a novel benchmark for generation and editing with over 1,700 challenging instances, and an accompanying evaluation metric, StructScore, which employs a multi-round Q\\\u0026amp;A protocol to assess fine-grained factual accuracy. Evaluations of 15 models reveal that even leading closed-source systems remain far from satisfactory. Our model attains strong editing performance, and inference-time reasoning yields consistent gains across diverse architectures. By releasing the dataset, model, and benchmark, we aim to advance unified multimodal foundations for structured visuals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026, Project page: https://structvisuals.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06218","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06218","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06218","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Deheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Runyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwen","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Tianwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guolei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Guolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajad","familyName":"Chhatkuli","name":"Chhatkuli, Ajad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Gang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yu-Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luc","familyName":"Van Gool","name":"Van Gool, Luc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danda Pani","familyName":"Paudel","name":"Paudel, Danda Pani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EgoNight: Towards Egocentric Vision Understanding at Night with a Challenging Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:04:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T11:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most existing benchmarks for understanding egocentric vision focus primarily on daytime scenarios, overlooking the low-light conditions that are inevitable in real-world applications. To investigate this gap, we present EgoNight, the first comprehensive benchmark for nighttime egocentric vision, with visual question answering (VQA) as the core task. A key feature of EgoNight is the introduction of day-night aligned videos, which enhance night annotation quality using the daytime data and reveal clear performance gaps between lighting conditions. To achieve this, we collect both synthetic videos rendered by Blender and real-world recordings, ensuring that scenes and actions are visually and temporally aligned. Leveraging these paired videos, we construct EgoNight-VQA, supported by a novel day-augmented night auto-labeling engine and refinement through extensive human verification. Each QA pair is double-checked by annotators for reliability. In total, EgoNight-VQA contains 3658 QA pairs across 90 videos, spanning 12 diverse QA types, with more than 300 hours of human work. Evaluations of state-of-the-art multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reveal substantial performance drops when transferring from day to night, underscoring the challenges of reasoning under low-light conditions. Beyond VQA, EgoNight also introduces two auxiliary tasks, day-night correspondence retrieval and egocentric depth estimation at night, that further explore the boundaries of existing models. We believe EgoNight-VQA provides a strong foundation for advancing application-driven egocentric vision research and for developing models that generalize across illumination domains. The code and data can be found at https://github.com/dehezhang2/EgoNight."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06218","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:09Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06203","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06203","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06203","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungeun","familyName":"Rho","name":"Rho, Seungeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Trinh","name":"Trinh, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danfei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Danfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sehoon","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Sehoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reference Grounded Skill Discovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:55:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-02T00:15:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:13:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T16:14:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:18:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling unsupervised skill discovery algorithms to high-DoF agents remains challenging. As dimensionality increases, the exploration space grows exponentially, while the manifold of meaningful skills remains limited. Therefore, semantic meaningfulness becomes essential to effectively guide exploration in high-dimensional spaces. In this work, we present Reference-Grounded Skill Discovery (RGSD), a novel algorithm that grounds skill discovery in a semantically meaningful latent space using reference data. RGSD first performs contrastive pretraining to embed motions on a unit hypersphere, clustering each reference trajectory into a distinct direction. This grounding enables skill discovery to simultaneously involve both imitation of reference behaviors and the discovery of semantically related diverse behaviors. On a simulated SMPL humanoid with $359$-D observations and $69$-D actions, RGSD successfully imitates skills such as walking, running, punching, and sidestepping, while also discover variations of these behaviors. In downstream locomotion tasks, RGSD leverages the discovered skills to faithfully satisfy user-specified style commands and outperforms imitation-learning baselines, which often fail to maintain the commanded style."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06203","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:47Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06005","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06005","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06005","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntian","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yuntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heming","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Heming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yunhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lianyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaohui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongrong","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Rongrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:06:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T09:29:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:39:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T11:39:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:10:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has emerged as a dominant method in Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) for large language models, which augments the transformer layer with one down-projection $A$ and one up-projection $B$. However, LoRA's reliance on a single down-projection matrix ($A$) creates a representational bottleneck, as this solitary feature extractor is inherently insufficient for capturing the diverse signals required by complex tasks. This motivates our architectural shift to focus on enriching the feature adaptation to improve the downstream task adaptation ability. We propose MASA (Multi-$A$ Shared Adaptation), an architecture that implements a multi-$A$, single-$B$ structure where the multi-$A$ expert ensemble is asymmetrically shared across layers to ensure parameter efficiency. In MASA, these specialized experts capture diverse features, which are then integrated by a single, layer-specific $B$-matrix. The effectiveness and versatility of our method are validated through a comprehensive suite of experiments spanning multi-domain generalization, single-domain specialization, and multi-task reasoning. For example, on the MMLU benchmark, MASA achieves an average accuracy of 59.62%, outperforming the standard LoRA by 1.08 points (a relative improvement of 1.84%) with comparable learnable parameters of 0.52%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06005","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:09Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva Prasad","familyName":"Kasiviswanathan","name":"Kasiviswanathan, Shiva Prasad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Large Language Models To Reason In Parallel With Global Forking Tokens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T07:00:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:25:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T00:48:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLMs have demonstrated improved performance by scaling parallel test-time compute, doing so relies on generating reasoning paths that are both diverse and accurate. For challenging problems, the forking tokens that trigger diverse yet correct reasoning modes are typically deep in the sampling tree. Consequently, common strategies to encourage diversity, such as temperature scaling, encounter a worsened trade-off between diversity and accuracy. Motivated by this challenge, we treat parallel reasoning as a set-of-next-token-prediction problem and incorporate a set-based global loss into Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) using bipartite matching between global forking tokens and unique reasoning traces. We observe that whereas naive fine-tuning with multiple reasoning traces collapses these unique reasoning modes, our proposed method, Set Supervised Fine-Tuning (SSFT), preserves these modes and produces emergent global forking tokens. Global Forking Policy Optimization (GFPO) leverages these maximally steerable tokens to incentivize complex reasoning, and the resulting models consistently outperform their SFT counterparts with GRPO on both math reasoning and execution-based code generation benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dachuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dachuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abedelkadir","familyName":"Asi","name":"Asi, Abedelkadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Keying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiangchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Leyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenke","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SwiReasoning: Switch-Thinking in Latent and Explicit for Pareto-Superior Reasoning LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T16:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T18:32:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work shows that, beyond discrete reasoning through explicit chain-of-thought steps, which are limited by the boundaries of natural languages, large language models (LLMs) can also reason continuously in latent space, allowing richer information per step and thereby improving token efficiency. Despite this promise, latent reasoning still faces two challenges, especially in training-free settings: 1) purely latent reasoning broadens the search distribution by maintaining multiple implicit paths, which diffuses probability mass, introduces noise, and impedes convergence to a single high-confidence solution, thereby hurting accuracy; and 2) overthinking persists even without explicit text, wasting tokens and degrading efficiency. To address these issues, we introduce SwiReasoning, a training-free framework for LLM reasoning which features two key innovations: 1) SwiReasoning dynamically switches between explicit and latent reasoning, guided by block-wise confidence estimated from entropy trends in next-token distributions, to balance exploration and exploitation and promote timely convergence. 2) By limiting the maximum number of thinking-block switches, SwiReasoning curbs overthinking and improves token efficiency across varying problem difficulties. On widely used mathematics, STEM, coding, and general benchmarks, SwiReasoning consistently improves average accuracy by 1.8%-3.1% across reasoning LLMs of different model families and scales. Furthermore, under constrained budgets, SwiReasoning improves average token efficiency by 57%-79%, with larger gains as budgets tighten."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/sdc17/SwiReasoning, Website: https://swireasoning.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05228","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05228","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05228","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haining","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Haining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James V.","familyName":"Roggeveen","name":"Roggeveen, James V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erez","familyName":"Berg","name":"Berg, Erez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Carrasquilla","name":"Carrasquilla, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debanjan","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Debanjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Surya","familyName":"Ganguli","name":"Ganguli, Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Ghimenti","name":"Ghimenti, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juraj","familyName":"Hasik","name":"Hasik, Juraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Hunt","name":"Hunt, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-Chen","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Hong-Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mason","familyName":"Kamb","name":"Kamb, Mason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying-Jer","familyName":"Kao","name":"Kao, Ying-Jer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Khatami","name":"Khatami, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Lawler","name":"Lawler, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Titus","familyName":"Neupert","name":"Neupert, Titus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael P.","familyName":"Brenner","name":"Brenner, Michael P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eun-Ah","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Eun-Ah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CMT-Benchmark: A Benchmark for Condensed Matter Theory Built by Expert Researchers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:00:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:22:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T16:22:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown remarkable progress in coding and math problem-solving, but evaluation on advanced research-level problems in hard sciences remains scarce. To fill this gap, we present CMT-Benchmark, a dataset of 50 problems covering condensed matter theory (CMT) at the level of an expert researcher. Topics span analytical and computational approaches in quantum many-body, and classical statistical mechanics. The dataset was designed and verified by a panel of expert researchers from around the world. We built the dataset through a collaborative environment that challenges the panel to write and refine problems they would want a research assistant to solve, including Hartree-Fock, exact diagonalization, quantum/variational Monte Carlo, density matrix renormalization group (DMRG), quantum/classical statistical mechanics, and model building. We evaluate LLMs by programmatically checking solutions against expert-supplied ground truth. We developed machine-grading, including symbolic handling of non-commuting operators via normal ordering. They generalize across tasks too. Our evaluations show that frontier models struggle with all of the problems in the dataset, highlighting a gap in the physical reasoning skills of current LLMs. Notably, experts identified strategies for creating increasingly difficult problems by interacting with the LLMs and exploiting common failure modes. The best model, GPT5, solves 30\\% of the problems; average across 17 models (GPT, Gemini, Claude, DeepSeek, Llama) is 11.4\\pm2.1\\%. Moreover, 18 problems are solved by none of the 17 models, and 26 by at most one. These unsolved problems span Quantum Monte Carlo, Variational Monte Carlo, and DMRG. Answers sometimes violate fundamental symmetries or have unphysical scaling dimensions. We believe this benchmark will guide development toward capable AI research assistants and tutors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CMT-Benchmark dataset is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/JVRoggeveen/cmt_benchmark. CMT-Benchmark was referenced in the Gemini 3 Deep Think (February 2026) release at https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-3-deep-think/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05228","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay Janapa","familyName":"Reddi","name":"Reddi, Vijay Janapa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slm-mux: Orchestrating small language models for reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T06:48:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development of language models, the number of small language models (SLMs) has grown significantly. Although they do not achieve state-of-the-art accuracy, they are more efficient and often excel at specific tasks. This raises a natural question: can multiple SLMs be orchestrated into a system where each contributes effectively, achieving higher accuracy than any individual model? Existing orchestration methods have primarily targeted frontier models (e.g., GPT-4) and perform suboptimally when applied to SLMs. To address this gap, we propose a three-stage approach for orchestrating SLMs. First, we introduce SLM-MUX, a multi-model architecture that effectively coordinates multiple SLMs. Building on this, we develop two optimization strategies: (i) a model selection search that identifies the most complementary SLMs from a given pool, and (ii) test-time scaling tailored to SLM-MUX. Our approach delivers strong results: Compared to existing orchestration methods, our approach achieves up to 13.4% improvement on MATH, 8.8% on GPQA, and 7.0% on GSM8K. With just two SLMs, SLM-MUX outperforms Qwen 2.5 72B on GPQA and GSM8K, and matches its performance on MATH. We further provide theoretical analyses to substantiate the advantages of our method. Additional experiments show that the core principle of SLM-MUX extends to open-ended generation tasks (e.g., HumanEval) and benefits other model classes, including frontier LLMs and domain-specific fine-tuned SLMs. In summary, we demonstrate that SLMs can be effectively orchestrated into more accurate and efficient systems through the proposed approach. The project page is available at https://slm-mux.github.io/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingdai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mingdai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurendra","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Nurendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangshu","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiangshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward W.","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Edward W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Subbian","name":"Subbian, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danai","familyName":"Koutra","name":"Koutra, Danai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentDR: Dynamic Recommendation with Implicit Item-Item Relations via LLM-based Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:48:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-22T15:17:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:52:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T12:32:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774904.3792304","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent agent-based recommendation frameworks aim to simulate user behaviors by incorporating memory mechanisms and prompting strategies, but they struggle with hallucinating non-existent items and full-catalog ranking. Besides, a largely underexplored opportunity lies in leveraging LLMs'commonsense reasoning to capture user intent through substitute and complement relationships between items, which are usually implicit in datasets and difficult for traditional ID-based recommenders to capture. In this work, we propose a novel LLM-agent framework, AgenDR, which bridges LLM reasoning with scalable recommendation tools. Our approach delegates full-ranking tasks to traditional models while utilizing LLMs to (i) integrate multiple recommendation outputs based on personalized tool suitability and (ii) reason over substitute and complement relationships grounded in user history. This design mitigates hallucination, scales to large catalogs, and enhances recommendation relevance through relational reasoning. Through extensive experiments on three public grocery datasets, we show that our framework achieves superior full-ranking performance, yielding on average a twofold improvement over its underlying tools. We also introduce a new LLM-based evaluation metric that jointly measures semantic alignment and ranking correctness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, accepted by WWW'26 as long paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaofeng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cihang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Cihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxiu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Huaxiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alignment Tipping Process: How Self-Evolution Pushes LLM Agents Off the Rails"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T20:59:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Model (LLM) agents increasingly gain self-evolutionary capabilities to adapt and refine their strategies through real-world interaction, their long-term reliability becomes a critical concern. We identify the Alignment Tipping Process (ATP), a critical post-deployment risk unique to self-evolving LLM agents. Unlike training-time failures, ATP arises when continual interaction drives agents to abandon alignment constraints established during training in favor of reinforced, self-interested strategies. We formalize and analyze ATP through two complementary paradigms: Self-Interested Exploration, where repeated high-reward deviations induce individual behavioral drift, and Imitative Strategy Diffusion, where deviant behaviors spread across multi-agent systems. Building on these paradigms, we construct controllable testbeds and benchmark both open and closed-source LLMs. Our experiments show that alignment benefits erode rapidly under self-evolution, with initially aligned models converging toward unaligned states. In multi-agent settings, successful violations diffuse quickly, leading to collective misalignment. Moreover, current reinforcement learning-based alignment methods provide limited defenses against alignment tipping. These findings demonstrate that alignment of LLM agents is not a static property but a fragile and dynamic one, vulnerable to feedback-driven decay during deployment. Our data and code are available at https://github.com/aiming-lab/ATP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peilin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Peilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keiji","familyName":"Yanai","name":"Yanai, Keiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HOI-R1: Exploring the Potential of Multimodal Large Language Models for Human-Object Interaction Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T03:07:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:59:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent human-object interaction detection (HOID) methods highly require prior knowledge from vision-language models (VLMs) to enhance the interaction recognition capabilities. The training strategies and model architectures for connecting the knowledge from VLMs to the HOI instance representations from the object detector are challenging, and the whole framework is complex for further development or application. On the other hand, the inherent reasoning abilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) on human-object interaction detection are under-explored. Inspired by the recent success of training MLLMs with reinforcement learning (RL) methods, we propose HOI-R1 and first explore the potential of the language model on the HOID task without any additional detection modules. We introduce an HOI reasoning process and HOID reward functions to solve the HOID task by pure text. Experiments on HICO-DET across multiple open-source MLLMs, including the Qwen-VL family (Qwen2.5-VL and Qwen3-VL) and Rex-Omni, show consistent improvements. Especially, HOI-R1 boosts Qwen2.5-VL-3B 2$\\times$ accuracy with great generalization ability. The source code is available at https://github.com/cjw2021/HOI-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zile","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Zile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Donglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanzhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuanzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minpeng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Minpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penguin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Penguin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne Xin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wayne Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihua","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Ruihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MARS: Co-evolving Dual-System Deep Research via Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:42:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:41:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T01:35:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:57:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) face two fundamental limitations: excessive token consumption when overanalyzing simple information processing tasks, and inability to access up-to-date knowledge beyond their training data. We introduce MARS (Multi-Agent System for Deep ReSearch), a novel co-evolution framework that jointly optimizes dual cognitive systems through multi-agent reinforcement learning. Unlike prior approaches that employ fixed or independently-trained summarizers, MARS enables System 1 (fast, intuitive processing) and System 2 (deliberate reasoning) to co-adapt through shared trajectory rewards, developing complementary strategies where System 1 learns to distill information specifically useful for System 2's reasoning. We extend Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) for multi-agent settings with three key innovations: (1) decoupled gradient computation ensuring proper credit assignment despite shared rewards, (2) bin-packing optimization for efficient parallel information processing, and (3) advantage-weighted balanced sampling preventing training imbalance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MARS (8B), trained under a challenging Zero RL setting without any supervised fine-tuning, achieves 8.17% on HLE -- outperforming WebThinker (32B with SFT, 6.87%) and narrowing the gap with proprietary models like Claude 3.7 Sonnet (7.89%) -- while achieving an average gain of 8.9% across 7 knowledge-intensive tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Ongoing Work"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:59:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:59:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05580","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05580","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05580","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhantao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Fangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anudeepsekhar","familyName":"Bolimera","name":"Bolimera, Anudeepsekhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marios","familyName":"Savvides","name":"Savvides, Marios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaVLA: Unified Meta Co-training For Efficient Embodied Adaption"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:54:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T08:17:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T01:37:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T03:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:19:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models show promise in embodied reasoning, yet remain far from true generalists-they often require task-specific fine-tuning, incur high compute costs, and generalize poorly to unseen tasks. We propose MetaVLA, a unified, backbone-agnostic post-training framework for efficient and scalable alignment. MetaVLA introduces Context-Aware Meta Co-Training, which consolidates diverse target tasks into a single fine-tuning stage while leveraging structurally diverse auxiliary tasks to improve in-domain generalization. Unlike naive multi-task SFT, MetaVLA integrates a lightweight meta-learning mechanism-derived from Attentive Neural Processes-to enable rapid adaptation from diverse contexts with minimal architectural change or inference overhead. On the LIBERO benchmark, MetaVLA with six auxiliary tasks outperforms OpenVLA by up to 8.0% on long-horizon tasks, reduces training steps from 240K to 75K, and cuts GPU time by ~76%. These results show that scalable, low-resource post-training is achievable-paving the way toward general-purpose embodied agents. Code will be available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05580","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:11Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-29T03:13:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05758","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05758","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05758","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Hanlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Taihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EMORL-TTS: Reinforcement Learning for Fine-Grained Emotion Control in LLM-based TTS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:24:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-24T06:22:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:17:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent LLM-based TTS systems achieve strong quality and zero-shot ability, but lack fine-grained emotional control due to their reliance on discrete speech tokens. Existing approaches either limit emotions to categorical labels or cannot generalize to LLM-based architectures. We propose EMORL-TTS (Fine-grained Emotion-controllable TTS with Reinforcement Learning), a framework that unifies global intensity control in the VAD space with local emphasis regulation. Our method combines supervised fine-tuning with reinforcement learning guided by task-specific rewards for emotion category, intensity, and emphasis. Moreover, we further investigate how emphasis placement modulates fine-grained emotion intensity. Experiments show that EMORL-TTS improves emotion accuracy, intensity differentiation, and emphasis clarity, while preserving synthesis quality comparable to strong LLM-based baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05758","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:19Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Lifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CARE: Cognitive-reasoning Augmented Reinforcement for Emotional Support Conversation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T03:19:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T13:55:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:38:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Emotional Support Conversation (ESC) plays a vital role in alleviating psychological stress and providing emotional value through dialogue. While recent studies have largely focused on data augmentation and synthetic corpus construction, they often overlook the deeper cognitive reasoning processes that underpin effective emotional support. To address this gap, we propose \\textbf{CARE}, a novel framework that strengthens reasoning in ESC without relying on large-scale synthetic data. CARE leverages the original ESC training set to guide models in generating logically coherent and supportive responses, thereby explicitly enhancing cognitive reasoning. Building on this foundation, we further employ reinforcement learning to refine and reinforce the reasoning process. Experimental results demonstrate that CARE significantly improves both the logical soundness and supportive quality of responses, advancing the development of empathetic, cognitively robust, and human-like emotional support systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:35Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nyal","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Nyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Bou","name":"Bou, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Jagota","name":"Jagota, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyapriya","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Satyapriya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonali","familyName":"Parbhoo","name":"Parbhoo, Sonali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning from Failures: Understanding LLM Alignment through Failure-Aware Inverse RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:20:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T10:13:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:25:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) aligns Large Language Models (LLMs) with human preferences, yet the underlying reward signals they internalize remain hidden, posing a critical challenge for interpretability and safety. Existing approaches attempt to extract these latent incentives using Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL), but treat all preference pairs equally, often overlooking the most informative signals: those examples the extracted reward model misclassifies or assigns nearly equal scores, which we term \\emph{failures}. We introduce a novel \\emph{failure-aware} IRL algorithm that focuses on misclassified or difficult examples to recover the latent rewards defining model behaviors. By learning from these failures, our failure-aware IRL extracts reward functions that better reflect the true objectives behind RLHF. We demonstrate that failure-aware IRL outperforms existing IRL baselines across multiple metrics when applied to LLM detoxification, without requiring external classifiers or supervision. Crucially, failure-aware IRL yields rewards that better capture the true incentives learned during RLHF, enabling more effective re-RLHF training than standard IRL. This establishes failure-aware IRL as a robust, scalable method for auditing model alignment and reducing ambiguity in the IRL process."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhejay","familyName":"Murali","name":"Murali, Abhejay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saleh","familyName":"Afroogh","name":"Afroogh, Saleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Atkinson","name":"Atkinson, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Dhurandhar","name":"Dhurandhar, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLM Safety Across Child Development Stages: A Simulated Agent Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T18:37:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current safety alignment for Large Language Models (LLMs) implicitly optimizes for a \"modal adult user,\" leaving models vulnerable to distributional shifts in user cognition. We present ChildSafe, a benchmark that quantifies alignment robustness under cognitive shifts corresponding to four developmental stages. Unlike static persona-based evaluations, we introduce a parametric cognitive simulation approach, formalizing developmental stages as hyperparameter constraints (e.g., volatility, context horizon) to generate out-of-distribution interaction traces. We validate these agents against ground-truth human linguistic data (CHILDES) and deploy them across 1,200 multi-turn interactions. Our results reveal a systematic alignment generalization gap: state-of-the-art models exhibit up to 11.5% performance degradation when interacting with early-childhood agents compared to standard baselines. We provide the research community with the validated agent artifacts and evaluation protocols to facilitate robust alignment testing against non-adversarial, cognitively diverse populations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ancheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiehui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longze","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Longze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmadreza","familyName":"Argha","name":"Argha, Ahmadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Alinejad-Rokny","name":"Alinejad-Rokny, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghuan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Minghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structuring Reasoning for Complex Rules Beyond Flat Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:10:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T12:09:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:15:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) face significant challenges when processing complex rule systems, as they typically treat interdependent rules as unstructured textual data rather than as logically organized frameworks. This limitation results in reasoning divergence, where models often overlook critical rule dependencies essential for accurate interpretation. Although existing approaches such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning have shown promise, they lack systematic methodologies for structured rule processing and are particularly susceptible to error propagation through sequential reasoning chains. To address these limitations, we propose the Dynamic Adjudication Template (DAT), a novel framework inspired by expert human reasoning processes. DAT structures the inference mechanism into three methodical stages: qualitative analysis, evidence gathering, and adjudication. During the qualitative analysis phase, the model comprehensively evaluates the contextual landscape. The subsequent evidence gathering phase involves the targeted extraction of pertinent information based on predefined template elements ([placeholder]), followed by systematic verification against applicable rules. Finally, in the adjudication phase, the model synthesizes these validated components to formulate a comprehensive judgment. Empirical results demonstrate that DAT consistently outperforms conventional CoT approaches in complex rule-based tasks. Notably, DAT enables smaller language models to match, and in some cases exceed, the performance of significantly larger LLMs, highlighting its efficiency and effectiveness in managing intricate rule systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianpeng","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jianpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-you","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hong-you","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satya Narayan","familyName":"Shukla","name":"Shukla, Satya Narayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijeet","familyName":"Awasthi","name":"Awasthi, Abhijeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xichen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaitanya","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Chaitanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shlok Kumar","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Shlok Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yonghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ser-Nam","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Ser-Nam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aashu","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Aashu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think Then Embed: Generative Context Improves Multimodal Embedding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T00:57:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T23:44:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T16:13:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There is a growing interest in Universal Multimodal Embeddings (UME), where models are required to generate task-specific representations. While recent studies show that Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) perform well on such tasks, they treat MLLMs solely as encoders, overlooking their generative capacity. However, such an encoding paradigm becomes less effective as instructions become more complex and require compositional reasoning. Inspired by the proven effectiveness of chain-of-thought reasoning, we propose a general Think-Then-Embed (TTE) framework for UME, composed of a reasoner and an embedder. The reasoner MLLM first generates reasoning traces that explain complex queries, followed by an embedder that produces representations conditioned on both the original query and the intermediate reasoning. This explicit reasoning step enables more nuanced understanding of complex multimodal instructions. Our contributions are threefold. First, by leveraging a powerful MLLM reasoner, we achieve state-of-the-art performance on the MMEB-V2 benchmark, surpassing proprietary models trained on massive in-house datasets. Second, to reduce the dependency on large MLLM reasoners, we finetune a smaller MLLM reasoner using high-quality embedding-centric reasoning traces, achieving the best performance among open-source models with a 7% absolute gain over recently proposed models. Third, we investigate strategies for integrating the reasoner and embedder into a unified model for improved efficiency without sacrificing performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:05:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Chieh","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cornelia","familyName":"Caragea","name":"Caragea, Cornelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MADIAVE: Multi-Agent Debate for Implicit Attribute Value Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:27:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T19:56:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit Attribute Value Extraction (AVE) is essential for accurately representing products in e-commerce, as it infers latent attributes from multimodal data. Despite advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs), implicit AVE remains challenging due to the complexity of multidimensional data and gaps in vision-text understanding. In this work, we introduce MADIAVE, a multi-agent debate framework that employs multiple MLLM agents to iteratively refine inferences. Through a series of debate rounds, agents verify and update each other's responses, thereby improving inference performance and robustness. Experiments on the ImplicitAVE dataset demonstrate that even a few rounds of debate significantly boost accuracy, especially for attributes with initially low performance. We systematically evaluate various debate configurations, including identical or different MLLM agents, and analyze how debate rounds affect convergence dynamics. Our findings highlight the potential of multi-agent debate strategies to address the limitations of single-agent approaches and offer a scalable solution for implicit AVE in multimodal e-commerce."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EACL 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06187","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06187","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06187","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Griffin","familyName":"Pitts","name":"Pitts, Griffin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aum","familyName":"Pandya","name":"Pandya, Aum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darsh","familyName":"Rank","name":"Rank, Darsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tirth","familyName":"Bhatt","name":"Bhatt, Tirth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muntasir","familyName":"Hoq","name":"Hoq, Muntasir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bita","familyName":"Akram","name":"Akram, Bita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Program Repair of Uncompilable Student Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:46:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T21:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T07:41:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-24T01:25:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3770761.3777323","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A significant portion of student programming submissions in CS1 learning environments are uncompilable, limiting their use in student modeling and downstream knowledge tracing. Traditional modeling pipelines often exclude these cases, discarding observations of student learning. This study investigates automated program repair as a strategy to recover uncompilable code while preserving students' structural intent for use in student modeling. Within this framework, we assess large language models (LLMs) as repair agents under high- and low-context prompting conditions. Repairs were evaluated for compilability, edit distance, and preservation of students' original structure and logic. While all models produced compilable repairs, they differed in how well they preserve students' control flow and code structure, affecting their pedagogical utility. By recovering uncompilable submissions, this work enables richer and more comprehensive analyses of learners' coding processes and development over time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In Proceedings of the 57th ACM Technical Symposium on Computer Science Education V.2 (SIGCSE TS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06187","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-24T02:56:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05137","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05137","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05137","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maojia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Maojia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renhang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Renhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dorien","familyName":"Herremans","name":"Herremans, Dorien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soujanya","familyName":"Poria","name":"Poria, Soujanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Demystifying deep search: a holistic evaluation with hint-free multi-hop questions and factorised metrics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:59:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T01:50:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:16:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T06:18:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-11T01:25:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) systems and web agents are increasingly evaluated on multi-hop deep search tasks, yet current practice suffers from two major limitations. First, most benchmarks leak the reasoning path in the question text, allowing models to follow surface cues rather than discover reasoning chains autonomously. Second, evaluation is typically reduced to a single pass rate, which collapses diverse behaviours into one score and obscures whether failures stem from inadequate search, poor knowledge use, or inappropriate refusal. To address these issues, we present WebDetective, a benchmark of hint-free multi-hop questions paired with a controlled Wikipedia sandbox that ensures full traceability of model actions, and a holistic evaluation framework that separates search sufficiency, knowledge utilisation, and refusal behaviour. Our evaluation of 25 state-of-the-art models reveals systematic weaknesses across all architectures: models struggle with knowledge utilisation despite having sufficient evidence and demonstrate near-absent appropriate refusal when evidence is lacking. These patterns expose a fundamental gap: today's systems excel at executing given reasoning paths but fail when required to discover them. We develop an agentic workflow, EvidenceLoop, that explicitly targets the challenges our benchmark identifies, incorporating verification loops and systematic evidence tracking that improve both search and synthesis capabilities. This baseline demonstrates that WebDetective's diagnostic framework can guide concrete architectural improvements, establishing our benchmark as a critical tool for developing genuinely autonomous reasoning systems rather than pattern-following agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05137","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-11T03:05:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Provably Mitigating Corruption, Overoptimization, and Verbosity Simultaneously in Offline and Online RLHF/DPO Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:32:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T17:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T02:00:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) and direct preference optimization (DPO) are important techniques to align large language models (LLM) with human preference. However, the quality of RLHF and DPO training is seriously compromised by \\textit{\\textbf{C}orrupted} preference, reward \\textit{\\textbf{O}veroptimization}, and bias towards \\textit{\\textbf{V}erbosity}. To our knowledge, most existing works tackle only one of these important issues, and the few other works require much computation to estimate multiple reward models and lack theoretical guarantee of generalization ability. In this work, we propose RLHF-\\textbf{COV} and DPO-\\textbf{COV} algorithms that can simultaneously mitigate these three issues, in both offline and online settings. This ability is theoretically demonstrated by obtaining length-regularized generalization error rates for our DPO-COV algorithms trained on corrupted data, which match the best-known rates for simpler cases with clean data and without length regularization. Moreover, our DPO-COV algorithm is simple to implement without reward estimation, and is proved to be equivalent to our RLHF-COV algorithm, which directly implies the equivalence between the vanilla RLHF and DPO algorithms. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our DPO-COV algorithms under both offline and online settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Edited a few incorrect numbers in Tables 2 and 3"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-10T03:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04996","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04996","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04996","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenlu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Chenlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baohao","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Baohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanze","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Hanze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xinxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christof","familyName":"Monz","name":"Monz, Christof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforce-Ada: An Adaptive Sampling Framework under Non-linear RL Objectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:34:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:57:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:16:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T17:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:47:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) for large language model reasoning is frequently hindered by signal loss, a phenomenon where standard uniform sampling with small group sizes fails to uncover informative learning signals for difficult prompts. We demonstrate that this collapse is a statistical artifact of undersampling rather than an inherent model limitation. To address this systematically, we introduce a theoretical framework based on optimizing a non-linear RL objective (e.g., log-likelihood). We show that this objective naturally induces a weighted gradient estimator that prioritizes difficult prompts, which can be robustly realized through adaptive sampling. Guided by this framework, we propose Reinforce-Ada, a family of algorithms that dynamically allocates inference budgets based on prompt difficulty, effectively scaling up RL compute to where it is needed most. Unlike passive filtering methods that discard low-signal prompts, Reinforce-Ada actively invests compute to recover them. We introduce two efficient realizations: an estimation-based approach and a model-free sequential sampling approach. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmarks show that Reinforce-Ada significantly outperforms uniform baselines like GRPO, recovering lost signals and accelerating convergence by up to $2\\times$ while maintaining the same total inference budget. Code is available at https://github.com/RLHFlow/Reinforce-Ada."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04996","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prateek","familyName":"Humane","name":"Humane, Prateek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Cudrano","name":"Cudrano, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel Z.","familyName":"Kaplan","name":"Kaplan, Daniel Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Matteucci","name":"Matteucci, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Supriyo","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Supriyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Rish","name":"Rish, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Influence Functions for Efficient Data Selection in Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:40:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T16:49:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:44:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) on chain-of-thought (CoT) data shows that a small amount of high-quality data can outperform massive datasets. Yet, what constitutes \"quality\" remains ill-defined. Existing reasoning methods rely on indirect heuristics such as problem difficulty or trace length, while instruction-tuning has explored a broader range of automated selection strategies, but rarely in the context of reasoning. We propose to define reasoning data quality using influence functions, which measure the causal effect of individual CoT examples on downstream accuracy, and introduce influence-based pruning, which consistently outperforms perplexity and embedding-based baselines on math reasoning within a model family."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 2 figures; added link to codebase"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05061","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05061","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05061","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasios","familyName":"Manganaris","name":"Manganaris, Anastasios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vittorio","familyName":"Giammarino","name":"Giammarino, Vittorio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed H.","familyName":"Qureshi","name":"Qureshi, Ahmed H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automaton Constrained Q-Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:38:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T08:39:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:42:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Real-world robotic tasks often require agents to achieve sequences of goals while respecting time-varying safety constraints. However, standard Reinforcement Learning (RL) paradigms are fundamentally limited in these settings. A natural approach to these problems is to combine RL with Linear-time Temporal Logic (LTL), a formal language for specifying complex, temporally extended tasks and safety constraints. Yet, existing RL methods for LTL objectives exhibit poor empirical performance in complex and continuous environments. As a result, no scalable methods support both temporally ordered goals and safety simultaneously, making them ill-suited for realistic robotics scenarios. We propose Automaton Constrained Q-Learning (ACQL), an algorithm that addresses this gap by combining goal-conditioned value learning with automaton-guided reinforcement. ACQL supports most LTL task specifications and leverages their automaton representation to explicitly encode stage-wise goal progression and both stationary and non-stationary safety constraints. We show that ACQL outperforms existing methods across a range of continuous control tasks, including cases where prior methods fail to satisfy either goal-reaching or safety constraints. We further validate its real-world applicability by deploying ACQL on a 6-DOF robotic arm performing a goal-reaching task in a cluttered, cabinet-like space with safety constraints. Our results demonstrate that ACQL is a robust and scalable solution for learning robotic behaviors according to rich temporal specifications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 main content pages, 4 main content figures, 11 appendix pages, 5 appendix figures, camera ready version submitted to 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05061","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yolo Y.","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yolo Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhangyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianxiang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiani","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Susan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhua","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Xing","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jia-Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bozheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Daiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyun","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ziyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Vosoughi","name":"Vosoughi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luchuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Luchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiki","familyName":"Shimada","name":"Shimada, Daiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiebo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jiebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenliang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-LMM Post-Training: A Deep Dive into Video Reasoning with Large Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T16:08:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:59:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:15:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T16:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:07:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-28T18:02:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:03:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-25T03:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:24:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video understanding represents the most challenging frontier in computer vision, requiring models to reason about complex spatiotemporal relationships, long-term dependencies, and multimodal evidence. The recent emergence of Video-Large Multimodal Models (Video-LMMs), which integrate visual encoders with powerful decoder-based language models, has demonstrated remarkable capabilities in video understanding tasks. However, the critical phase that transforms these models from basic perception systems into sophisticated reasoning engines, post-training, remains fragmented across the literature. This survey provides the first comprehensive examination of post-training methodologies for Video-LMMs, encompassing three fundamental pillars: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with chain-of-thought, reinforcement learning (RL) from verifiable objectives, and test-time scaling (TTS) through enhanced inference computation. We present a structured taxonomy that clarifies the roles, interconnections, and video-specific adaptations of these techniques, addressing unique challenges such as temporal localization, spatiotemporal grounding, long video efficiency, and multimodal evidence integration. Through systematic analysis of representative methods, we synthesize key design principles, insights, and evaluation protocols while identifying critical open challenges in reward design, scalability, and cost-performance optimization. We further curate essential benchmarks, datasets, and metrics to facilitate rigorous assessment of post-training effectiveness. This survey aims to provide researchers and practitioners with a unified framework for advancing Video-LMM capabilities. Additional resources and updates are maintained at: https://github.com/yunlong10/Awesome-Video-LMM-Post-Training"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version v1.1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-26T03:24:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Cai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Wipf","name":"Wipf, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Powerful Ways to Generate: Autoregression, Diffusion, and Beyond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T03:58:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:22:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models have recently emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive language models. Beyond next-token generation, they are more efficient and flexible by enabling parallel and any-order token generation. However, despite empirical successes, their computational power and fundamental limitations remain poorly understood. In this paper, we formally study whether non-autoregressive generation in Masked Diffusion Models (MDM) enables solving problems beyond the reach of Auto-Regressive Models (ARM). Our results show that MDM with sufficiently large context length is computationally universal with decoding steps matching the optimal parallel time complexity in PRAM. However, when controlling for other factors, MDM's flexibility to generate in any-order does not expand what ARM can already solve. To address this, we propose a new form of generation called any-process generation, which extends MDM with capabilities to remask, insert and delete tokens, allowing self-correction, length-variable editing, and adaptive parallelism. Theoretically and empirically, we demonstrate these capabilities enable scalability to significantly harder reasoning problems that are otherwise intractable for ARM and vanilla MDM. Additionally, they prove essential for generation tasks where objects naturally evolve through non-sequential processes, crucial for extending current LLMs beyond natural language to domains such as coding and science."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Python","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Python","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke Tenyi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Luke Tenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Yun","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Yun-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Penghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning under Vision: Understanding Visual-Spatial Cognition in Vision-Language Models for CAPTCHA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:56:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-16T00:34:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:48:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CAPTCHA, originally designed to distinguish humans from robots, has evolved into a real-world benchmark for assessing the spatial reasoning capabilities of vision-language models. In this work, we first show that step-by-step reasoning is crucial for vision-language models (VLMs) to solve CAPTCHAs, which represent high-difficulty spatial reasoning tasks, and that current commercial vision-language models still struggle with such reasoning. In particular, we observe that most commercial VLMs (e.g., Gemini, Claude, GPT, etc.) fail to effectively solve CAPTCHAs and thus achieve low accuracy (around 21.9 percent). However, our findings indicate that requiring the model to perform step-by-step reasoning before generating the final coordinates can significantly enhance its solving accuracy, underscoring the severity of the gap. To systematically study this issue, we introduce CAPTCHA-X, the first real-world CAPTCHA benchmark with reasoning, covering seven categories of CAPTCHAs (such as Gobang, hCaptcha, etc.) with step-by-step action solutions and grounding annotations. We further define five reasoning-oriented metrics that enable a comprehensive evaluation of models reasoning capabilities. To validate the effectiveness of reasoning, we also propose a general agentic VLM-based framework that incorporates the models inherent reasoning abilities. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance across five high-difficulty CAPTCHA types, with an average solving accuracy of 83.9 percent, substantially surpassing existing baselines. These results reveal the limitations of current models and highlight the importance of reasoning in advancing visual-spatial challenges in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14pages, 11figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry Peng","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Henry Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangning","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yankai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wooseong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wooseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinni","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dianzhi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang H","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Hoang H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhou","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhe","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wenzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chin-Yuan","familyName":"Yeh","name":"Yeh, Chin-Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liancheng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Liancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhu","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Jinhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Chieh","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyao","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zhengyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Langzhou","familyName":"He","name":"He, Langzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai-Tao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hai-Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irwin","familyName":"King","name":"King, Irwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RECODE-H: A Benchmark for Research Code Development with Interactive Human Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:45:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T17:20:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) show the promise in supporting scientific research implementation, yet their ability to generate correct and executable code remains limited. Existing works largely adopt one-shot settings, ignoring the iterative and feedback-driven nature of realistic workflows of scientific research development. To address this gap, we present RECODE-H, a benchmark of 102 tasks from research papers and repositories that evaluates LLM agents through multi-turn interactions with LLM-simulated human feedback. It includes structured instructions,unit tests, and a five-level feedback hierarchy to reflect realistic researcher-agent collaboration. We further present ReCodeAgent, a framework that integrates feedback into iterative code generation. Experiments with leading LLMs, including GPT-5, Claude-Sonnet-4, DeepSeek-V3.1, and Gemini 2.5, show substantial performance gains with richer feedback, while also highlighting ongoing challenges in the generation of complex research code. RECODE-H establishes a foundation for developing adaptive, feedback-driven LLM agents in scientific research implementation"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and dataset are available at github.com/ChunyuMiao98/RECODE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:12:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chengzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhe","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin Eric","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Presenting a Paper is an Art: Self-Improvement Aesthetic Agents for Academic Presentations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:24:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T21:04:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:09:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The promotion of academic papers has become an important means of enhancing research visibility. However, existing automated methods struggle limited storytelling, insufficient aesthetic quality, and constrained self-adjustment, making it difficult to achieve efficient and engaging dissemination. At the heart of those challenges is a simple principle: \\emph{there is no way to improve it when you cannot evaluate it right}. To address this, we introduce \\textbf{EvoPresent}, a self-improvement agent framework that unifies coherent narratives, aesthetic-aware designs, and realistic presentation delivery via virtual characters. Central to EvoPresent is \\textbf{PresAesth}, a multi-task reinforcement learning (RL) aesthetic model that provides reliable aesthetic scoring, defect adjustment, and comparative feedback, enabling iterative self-improvement even under limited aesthetic training data. To systematically evaluate the methods, we introduce \\textbf{EvoPresent Benchmark}, a comprehensive benchmark comprising: \\textit{Presentation Generation Quality}, built on 650 top-tier AI conference papers with multimodal resources (slides, videos and scripts) to assess both content and design; and \\textit{Aesthetic Awareness}, consisting of 2,000 slide pairs with varying aesthetic levels, supporting joint training and evaluation on scoring, defect adjustment, and comparison. Our findings highlight that (i) High-quality feedback is essential for agent self-improvement, while initial capability alone does not guarantee effective self-correction. (ii) Automated generation pipelines exhibit a trade-off between visual design and content construction. (iii) Multi-task RL training shows stronger generalization in aesthetic awareness tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aksel Joonas","familyName":"Reedi","name":"Reedi, Aksel Joonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Corentin","familyName":"Léger","name":"Léger, Corentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Pourcel","name":"Pourcel, Julien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Loris","familyName":"Gaven","name":"Gaven, Loris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Perrine","familyName":"Charriau","name":"Charriau, Perrine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Pourcel","name":"Pourcel, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing for Persuasion Improves LLM Generalization: Evidence from Quality-Diversity Evolution of Debate Strategies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:20:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T14:06:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:31:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) optimized to output truthful answers often overfit, producing brittle reasoning that fails to generalize. While persuasion-based optimization has shown promise in debate settings, it has not been systematically compared against mainstream truth-based approaches. We introduce DebateQD, a minimal Quality-Diversity (QD) evolutionary algorithm that evolves diverse debate strategies across different categories (rationality, authority, emotional appeal, etc.) through tournament-style competitions where two LLMs debate while a third judges. Unlike previously proposed methods that require a population of LLMs, our approach maintains diversity of opponents through prompt-based strategies within a single LLM architecture, making it more accessible for experiments while preserving the key benefits of population-based optimization. In contrast to prior work, we explicitly isolate the role of the optimization objective by fixing the debate protocol and swapping only the fitness function: persuasion rewards strategies that convince the judge irrespective of truth, whereas truth rewards collaborative correctness. Across three model scales (7B, 32B, 72B parameters) and multiple dataset sizes from the QuALITY benchmark, persuasion-optimized strategies achieve up to 13.94% smaller train-test generalization gaps, while matching or exceeding truth optimization's test performance. These results provide the first controlled evidence that competitive pressure to persuade, rather than seek the truth collaboratively, fosters more transferable reasoning skills, offering a promising path for improving LLM generalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Open-source code available at https://github.com/flowersteam/llm_persuasion"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adi","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Adi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Nair","name":"Nair, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarik","familyName":"Borogovac","name":"Borogovac, Tarik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Did It All Go Wrong? A Hierarchical Look into Multi-Agent Error Attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:07:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T18:25:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:03:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Error attribution in Large Language Model (LLM) multi-agent systems presents a significant challenge in debugging and improving collaborative AI systems. Current approaches to pinpointing agent and step level failures in interaction traces - whether using all-at-once evaluation, step-by-step analysis, or binary search - fall short when analyzing complex patterns, struggling with both accuracy and consistency. We present ECHO (Error attribution through Contextual Hierarchy and Objective consensus analysis), a novel algorithm that combines hierarchical context representation, objective analysis-based evaluation, and consensus voting to improve error attribution accuracy. Our approach leverages a positional-based leveling of contextual understanding while maintaining objective evaluation criteria, ultimately reaching conclusions through a consensus mechanism. Experimental results demonstrate that ECHO outperforms existing methods across various multi-agent interaction scenarios, showing particular strength in cases involving subtle reasoning errors and complex interdependencies. Our findings suggest that leveraging these concepts of structured, hierarchical context representation combined with consensus-based objective decision-making, provides a more robust framework for error attribution in multi-agent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:02:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05764","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05764","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05764","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lang","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yankai","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaishun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kaishun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jintai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jintai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RareAgent: Self-Evolving Reasoning for Drug Repurposing in Rare Diseases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T03:52:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:24:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computational drug repurposing for rare diseases is especially challenging when no prior associations exist between drugs and target diseases. Therefore, knowledge graph completion and message-passing GNNs have little reliable signal to learn and propagate, resulting in poor performance. We present RareAgent, a self-evolving multi-agent system that reframes this task from passive pattern recognition to active evidence-seeking reasoning. RareAgent organizes task-specific adversarial debates in which agents dynamically construct evidence graphs from diverse perspectives to support, refute, or entail hypotheses. The reasoning strategies are analyzed post hoc in a self-evolutionary loop, producing textual feedback that refines agent policies, while successful reasoning paths are distilled into transferable heuristics to accelerate future investigations. Comprehensive evaluations reveal that RareAgent improves the indication AUPRC by 18.1% over reasoning baselines and provides a transparent reasoning chain consistent with clinical evidence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05764","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:19:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zefu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zefu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongxu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Rongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Hanning","name":"Hanning, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojuan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiaojuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wenbo","name":"Wenbo, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lue","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EmbodiedCoder: Parameterized Embodied Mobile Manipulation via Modern Coding Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T01:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in control robot methods, from end-to-end vision-language-action frameworks to modular systems with predefined primitives, have advanced robots' ability to follow natural language instructions. Nonetheless, many approaches still struggle to scale to diverse environments, as they often rely on large annotated datasets and offer limited interpretability.In this work, we introduce EmbodiedCoder, a training-free framework for open-world mobile robot manipulation that leverages coding models to directly generate executable robot trajectories. By grounding high-level instructions in code, EmbodiedCoder enables flexible object geometry parameterization and manipulation trajectory synthesis without additional data collection or fine-tuning.This coding-based paradigm provides a transparent and generalizable way to connect perception with manipulation. Experiments on real mobile robots show that EmbodiedCoder achieves robust performance across diverse long-term tasks and generalizes effectively to novel objects and environments.Our results demonstrate an interpretable approach for bridging high-level reasoning and low-level control, moving beyond fixed primitives toward versatile robot intelligence. See the project page at: https://embodiedcoder.github.io/EmbodiedCoder/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Demo Page: https://embodiedcoder.github.io/EmbodiedCoder/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:53Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05839","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05839","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05839","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Robust and Realible Multimodal Misinformation Recognition with Incomplete Modality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:03:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T12:48:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:54:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:45:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:19:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Misinformation Recognition has become an urgent task with the emergence of huge multimodal fake content on social media platforms. Previous studies mainly focus on complex feature extraction and fusion to learn discriminative information from multimodal content. However, in real-world applications, multimedia news may naturally lose some information during dissemination, resulting in modality incompleteness, which is detrimental to the generalization and robustness of existing models. To this end, we propose a novel generic and robust multimodal fusion strategy, termed Multi-expert Modality-incomplete Learning Network (MMLNet), which is simple yet effective. It consists of three key steps: (1) Multi-Expert Collaborative Reasoning to compensate for missing modalities by dynamically leveraging complementary information through multiple experts. (2) Incomplete Modality Adapters compensates for the missing information by leveraging the new feature distribution. (3) Modality Missing Learning leveraging an label-aware adaptive weighting strategy to learn a robust representation with contrastive learning. We evaluate MMLNet on three real-world benchmarks across two languages, demonstrating superior performance compared to state-of-the-art methods while maintaining relative simplicity. By ensuring the accuracy of misinformation recognition in incomplete modality scenarios caused by information propagation, MMLNet effectively curbs the spread of malicious misinformation. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/zhyhome/MMLNet."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05839","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:22:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaochen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Gaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:46:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:22:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in language agents have led to significant improvements in multi-hop reasoning tasks. However, existing approaches often struggle with handling open-domain problems, which require massive information retrieval due to their reliance on a fixed sequence of actions. To address this, we propose Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning (FGDIP), a novel framework tailored to enhance reasoning in LLMs by utilizing dynamic and adaptive strategies for information exploration in open-domain multi-hop reasoning tasks. Our approach begins by identifying key entities relevant to the problem, which serve as the initial nodes in the reasoning process. From these initial nodes, we then generate reasoning child nodes with the process being refined through a combination of historical error analysis and real-time feedback, which allows the framework to dynamically adjust and optimize its reasoning strategies. By integrating depth-first search with an innovative node generation technique, our framework adapts based on both prior error paths and concurrently generated nodes at the same hierarchical level. This dynamic strategy effectively expands the search space while ensuring the reasoning process systematically converges toward accurate solutions. Experimental results show that FGDIP achieved up to 54.47% F1 score on the HotpotQA dataset and 70.05% on the StrategyQA dataset, surpassing the best baseline by 5.03% and 7.25% respectively, highlighting its versatility and potential to enhance language agents in multi-hop reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05330","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05330","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05330","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linji","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Linji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuesu","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xuesu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Dynamics Planning for Robot Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:08:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T20:19:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous robot navigation systems often rely on hierarchical planning, where global planners compute collision-free paths without considering dynamics, and local planners enforce dynamics constraints to produce executable commands. This discontinuity in dynamics often leads to trajectory tracking failure in highly constrained environments. Recent approaches integrate dynamics within the entire planning process by gradually decreasing its fidelity, e.g., increasing integration steps and reducing collision checking resolution, for real-time planning efficiency. However, they assume that the fidelity of the dynamics should decrease according to a manually designed scheme. Such static settings fail to adapt to environmental complexity variations, resulting in computational overhead in simple environments or insufficient dynamics consideration in obstacle-rich scenarios. To overcome this limitation, we propose Adaptive Dynamics Planning (ADP), a learning-augmented paradigm that uses reinforcement learning to dynamically adjust robot dynamics properties, enabling planners to adapt across diverse environments. We integrate ADP into three different planners and further design a standalone ADP-based navigation system, benchmarking them against other baselines. Experiments in both simulation and real-world tests show that ADP consistently improves navigation success, safety, and efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05330","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Kondo","name":"Kondo, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. P.","familyName":"How","name":"How, J. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Navigation in Unknown Indoor Environments with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:26:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T16:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel high-level planning framework that leverages vision-language models (VLMs) to improve autonomous navigation in unknown indoor environments with many dead ends. Traditional exploration methods often take inefficient routes due to limited global reasoning and reliance on local heuristics. In contrast, our approach enables a VLM to reason directly about occupancy maps in a zero-shot manner, selecting subgoals that are likely to yield more efficient paths. At each planning step, we convert a 3D occupancy grid into a partial 2D map of the environment, and generate candidate subgoals. Each subgoal is then evaluated and ranked against other candidates by the model. We integrate this planning scheme into DYNUS \\cite{kondo2025dynus}, a state-of-the-art trajectory planner, and demonstrate improved navigation efficiency in simulation. The VLM infers structural patterns (e.g., rooms, corridors) from incomplete maps and balances the need to make progress toward a goal against the risk of entering unknown space. This reduces common greedy failures (e.g., detouring into small rooms) and achieves about 10\\% shorter paths on average."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures, accepted to the OWN workshop at IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jike","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Psounis","name":"Psounis, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context Matters: Learning Global Semantics via Object-Centric Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:33:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T18:28:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:03:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in language modeling have witnessed the rise of highly desirable emergent capabilities, such as reasoning and in-context learning. However, vision models have yet to exhibit comparable progress in these areas. In this paper, we argue that this gap could stem from the lack of semantic and contextual guidance in current vision transformer (ViT) training schemes, and such a gap can be narrowed through the design of a semantic-grounded objective. Specifically, we notice that individual words in natural language are inherently semantic, and modeling directly on word tokens naturally learns a realistic distribution. In contrast, ViTs rely on spatial patchification, which inevitably lacks semantic information. To bridge this gap, we propose to directly model \"object\" as the visual equivalence of \"word,\" pushing the model to learn the global context and semantics among visual elements. We investigate our hypotheses via masked image modeling (MIM), a framework where our approach can be readily tested by applying masks to visual objects rather than random patches. Considerable evidence from qualitative and quantitative evaluations reveals a key finding: object-level representation alone helps to learn a real-world distribution, whereas pixel-averaging shortcuts are often learned without it. Moreover, further evaluations with multimodal LLMs (MLLM) on visual question answering (VQA, GQA, ScienceQA) tasks demonstrate the strong reasoning and contextual understanding gained with this simple objective. We hope our study highlights the effectiveness of object-level encoding and provides a plausible direction for developing stronger vision encoders and tokenizers. Code and model will be publicly released. Keywords: Semantic Visual Tokenizer, Vision Reasoning, In-context Learning, Multimodal Reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Diederich","name":"Diederich, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rule Encoding and Compliance in Large Language Models: An Information-Theoretic Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T14:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T09:08:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:41:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The design of safety-critical agents based on large language models (LLMs) requires more than simple prompt engineering. This paper presents a comprehensive information-theoretic analysis of how rule encodings in system prompts influence attention mechanisms and compliance behaviour. We demonstrate that rule formats with low syntactic entropy and highly concentrated anchors reduce attention entropy and improve pointer fidelity, but reveal a fundamental trade-off between anchor redundancy and attention entropy that previous work failed to recognize. Through formal analysis of multiple attention architectures including causal, bidirectional, local sparse, kernelized, and cross-attention mechanisms, we establish bounds on pointer fidelity and show how anchor placement strategies must account for competing fidelity and entropy objectives. Combining these insights with a dynamic rule verification architecture, we provide a formal proof that hot reloading of verified rule sets increases the asymptotic probability of compliant outputs. These findings underscore the necessity of principled anchor design and dual enforcement mechanisms to protect LLM-based agents against prompt injection attacks while maintaining compliance in evolving domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin Qinghong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kevin Qinghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike Zheng","familyName":"Shou","name":"Shou, Mike Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Paper2Video: Automatic Video Generation from Scientific Papers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:29:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:11:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Academic presentation videos have become an essential medium for research communication, yet producing them remains highly labor-intensive, often requiring hours of slide design, recording, and editing for a short 2 to 10 minutes video. Unlike natural video, presentation video generation involves distinctive challenges: inputs from research papers, dense multi-modal information (text, figures, tables), and the need to coordinate multiple aligned channels such as slides, subtitles, speech, and human talker. To address these challenges, we introduce Paper2Video, the first benchmark of 101 research papers paired with author-created presentation videos, slides, and speaker metadata. We further design four tailored evaluation metrics--Meta Similarity, PresentArena, PresentQuiz, and IP Memory--to measure how videos convey the paper's information to the audience. Building on this foundation, we propose PaperTalker, the first multi-agent framework for academic presentation video generation. It integrates slide generation with effective layout refinement by a novel effective tree search visual choice, cursor grounding, subtitling, speech synthesis, and talking-head rendering, while parallelizing slide-wise generation for efficiency. Experiments on Paper2Video demonstrate that the presentation videos produced by our approach are more faithful and informative than existing baselines, establishing a practical step toward automated and ready-to-use academic video generation. Our dataset, agent, and code are available at https://github.com/showlab/Paper2Video."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://showlab.github.io/Paper2Video/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:06:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:06:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jim","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Jim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manxi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Manxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Batched Scheduling of Stochastic Processing Networks Using Atomic Action Decomposition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:31:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T02:33:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:19:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Stochastic processing networks (SPNs) have broad applications in healthcare, transportation, and communication networks. The control of SPN is to dynamically assign servers in batches under uncertainty to optimize long-run performance. This problem is challenging as the policy dimension grows exponentially with the number of servers, making standard reinforcement learning and policy optimization methods intractable at scale. We propose an atomic action decomposition framework that addresses this scalability challenge by breaking joint assignments into sequential single-server assignments. This yields policies with constant dimension, independent of the number of servers. We study two classes of atomic policies, the step-dependent and step-independent atomic policies, and prove that both achieve the same optimal long-run average reward as the original joint policies. These results establish that computing the optimal SPN control can be made scalable without loss of optimality using the atomic framework. Our results offer theoretical justification for the strong empirical success of the atomic framework in large-scale applications reported in previous articles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaogeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoDAN-Reasoning: Enhancing Strategies Exploration based Jailbreak Attacks with Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:16:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:11:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in jailbreaking large language models (LLMs), such as AutoDAN-Turbo, have demonstrated the power of automated strategy discovery. AutoDAN-Turbo employs a lifelong learning agent to build a rich library of attack strategies from scratch. While highly effective, its test-time generation process involves sampling a strategy and generating a single corresponding attack prompt, which may not fully exploit the potential of the learned strategy library. In this paper, we propose to further improve the attack performance of AutoDAN-Turbo through test-time scaling. We introduce two distinct scaling methods: Best-of-N and Beam Search. The Best-of-N method generates N candidate attack prompts from a sampled strategy and selects the most effective one based on a scorer model. The Beam Search method conducts a more exhaustive search by exploring combinations of strategies from the library to discover more potent and synergistic attack vectors. According to the experiments, the proposed methods significantly boost performance, with Beam Search increasing the attack success rate by up to 15.6 percentage points on Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct and achieving a nearly 60% relative improvement against the highly robust GPT-o4-mini compared to the vanilla method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report. Code is available at https://github.com/SaFoLab-WISC/AutoDAN-Reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Habibi","name":"Habibi, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung Wan","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Seung Wan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atieh","familyName":"Kashani","name":"Kashani, Atieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ala","familyName":"Shafia","name":"Shafia, Ala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lakshana","familyName":"Lakshmanarajan","name":"Lakshmanarajan, Lakshana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Fang","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Chia-Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magy Seif","familyName":"El-Nasr","name":"El-Nasr, Magy Seif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Do You Mean? Exploring How Humans and AI Interact with Symbols and Meanings in Their Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:13:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T13:52:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Meaningful human-AI collaboration requires more than processing language; it demands a deeper understanding of symbols and their socially constructed meanings. While humans naturally interpret symbols through social interaction, AI systems often miss the dynamic interpretations that emerge in conversation. Drawing on Symbolic Interactionism theory, we conducted two studies to investigate how humans and AI co-construct symbols and their meanings. Findings provide empirical insights into how humans and conversational AI agents collaboratively shape meanings during interaction. We show how participants shift their initial definitions of meaning in response to the symbols and interpretations suggested by the conversational AI agents, especially when social context is introduced. We also observe how participants project their personal and social values into these interactions, refining meanings over time. These findings reveal that shared understanding does not emerge from mere agreement but from the bi-directional exchange and reinterpretation of symbols, suggesting new paradigms for human-AI interaction design."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CHI 2026 Papers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas Carrit Delgado","familyName":"Pinheiro","name":"Pinheiro, Lucas Carrit Delgado","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziru","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno Caixeta","familyName":"Piazza","name":"Piazza, Bruno Caixeta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ness","familyName":"Shroff","name":"Shroff, Ness","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingbin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yingbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan-Sen","familyName":"Ting","name":"Ting, Yuan-Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Achieve Gold Medal Performance at the International Olympiad on Astronomy \u0026amp; Astrophysics (IOAA)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:58:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T15:34:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While task-specific demonstrations show early success in applying large language models (LLMs) to automate some astronomical research tasks, they only provide incomplete views of all necessary capabilities in solving astronomy problems, calling for more thorough understanding of LLMs' strengths and limitations. So far, existing benchmarks and evaluations focus on simple question-answering that primarily tests astronomical knowledge and fails to evaluate the complex reasoning required for real-world research in the discipline. Here, we address this gap by systematically benchmarking five state-of-the-art LLMs on the International Olympiad on Astronomy and Astrophysics (IOAA) exams, which are designed to examine deep conceptual understanding, multi-step derivations, and multimodal analysis. With average scores of 85.6% and 84.2%, Gemini 2.5 Pro and GPT-5 (the two top-performing models) not only achieve gold medal level performance but also rank in the top two among ~200-300 participants in all four IOAA theory exams evaluated (2022-2025). In comparison, results on the data analysis exams show more divergence. GPT-5 still excels in the exams with an 88.5% average score, ranking top 10 among the participants in the four most recent IOAAs, while other models' performances drop to 48-76%. Furthermore, our in-depth error analysis underscores conceptual reasoning, geometric reasoning, and spatial visualization (52-79% accuracy) as consistent weaknesses among all LLMs. Hence, although LLMs approach peak human performance in theory exams, critical gaps must be addressed before they can serve as autonomous research agents in astronomy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 6 figures, to be submitted, comments are welcome. Reproducibility details can be found at: https://github.com/OSU-NLP-Group/LLM-IOAA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ailiya","familyName":"Borjigin","name":"Borjigin, Ailiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Safe and Compliant Cross-Market Trade Execution via Constrained RL and Zero-Knowledge Audits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:42:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T07:12:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:28:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a cross-market algorithmic trading system that balances execution quality with rigorous compliance enforcement. The architecture comprises a high-level planner, a reinforcement learning execution agent, and an independent compliance agent. We formulate trade execution as a constrained Markov decision process with hard constraints on participation limits, price bands, and self-trading avoidance. The execution agent is trained with proximal policy optimization, while a runtime action-shield projects any unsafe action into a feasible set. To support auditability without exposing proprietary signals, we add a zero-knowledge compliance audit layer that produces cryptographic proofs that all actions satisfied the constraints. We evaluate in a multi-venue, ABIDES-based simulator and compare against standard baselines (e.g., TWAP, VWAP). The learned policy reduces implementation shortfall and variance while exhibiting no observed constraint violations across stress scenarios including elevated latency, partial fills, compliance module toggling, and varying constraint limits. We report effects at the 95% confidence level using paired t-tests and examine tail risk via CVaR. We situate the work at the intersection of optimal execution, safe reinforcement learning, regulatory technology, and verifiable AI, and discuss ethical considerations, limitations (e.g., modeling assumptions and computational overhead), and paths to real-world deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:00:05Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:00:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":207,"totalPages":3,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22+OR+reasoning+OR+%22reinforcement+learning%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22+OR+reasoning+OR+%22reinforcement+learning%22%29"}}